WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.620
- Hi everyone.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.840
Thank so much for coming, I'm Steve Pitti,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.020
I'm the professor of
History and American Studies

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.080
and director of the Center
for the Study of Race,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.710
Indigeneity, and Transnational Migration

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.750
here at Yale, thank you everybody

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.170
for turning out for this
wonderful opportunity

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.530
to hear from one of my
very favorite scholars,

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.460
Professor Ana Minian
of Stanford University.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.760
I wanna thank Nandi Cummings and others

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.000
at the RITM Center for helping to put this

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.671
event together and making
all of this possible.

00:00:39.671 --> 00:00:42.730
Ana Minian is assistant
professor of history

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:46.610
at Stanford, she received her BA degree

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.870
from the University of Chicago

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.500
and then her masters
and her PhD, the latter

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.190
in 2012 from Yale's
American Studies program

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.097
here in New Haven, her dissertation

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:02.970
which is the basis for her tremendous book

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.230
from which she's speaking today

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.120
and which you can see
pictured on the screen

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.370
next to me, received in 2012,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:14.370
the Ralph Henry Gabriel
Prize for, as the best

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.343
dissertation written in American Studies,

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.400
in the field of American
Studies in that year.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.950
Ana, as you'll see today, is a top scholar

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.960
of US History and of Mexican History,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.750
especially in the late 20th Century,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.461
and to my mind, is the finest historian

00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:35.890
of Mexican migration working today.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.680
She's published in the
Journal American Quarterly

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.490
and the Journal of American History,

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.090
in places like the Washington
Post and elsewhere.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.940
She's also made other
important media appearances

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.470
in recent months, talking about issues

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.640
related to Mexican migration and migration

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.596
in Mexico, her book, Undocumented Lives,

00:01:54.596 --> 00:01:58.730
published just months ago
by Harvard University Press,

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:03.350
in 2018, explores the
late 20th Century history

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.820
of Mexican undocumented migration

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.580
to the United States, the growth

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.900
of migrant communities
in the United States

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.740
and by national efforts,
in the late 20th Century

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.260
to regulate the US/Mexico border.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.860
Ana, in writing this history, relies upon

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.193
an incredible set of
sources, of primary sources.

00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:28.940
Many of which, she's the first to use

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.873
as a scholar, she did
over 200 oral histories

00:02:32.873 --> 00:02:35.230
to do this project, she worked with

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.360
Government archives, she captured

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.100
migrant correspondents letters

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.430
and also used privately
held organizational

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.150
records and personal collections,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.060
pamphlets and unpublished ephemera,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.260
and newspapers and magazines collected

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.530
in places like Washington DC, Chicago,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.680
the San Francisco Bay area, Los Angeles,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.890
Michoacán, Zacatecas and Mexico City.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.046
What she crafted, is really the first

00:03:06.046 --> 00:03:09.100
sustained history of transnational

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.180
Mexican migration from roughly,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.020
the year 1965 to 1986, and what she does

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.010
in this book, Undocumented Lives,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.600
should, I think, reset how we talk about

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.070
the recent histories of both Mexico

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.440
and the United States,
indeed of governments,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.860
of every day people, of migrants

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.000
and of non-migrants, of what it means

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.830
to be a way journeyer, of what it means

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.943
to be a family member, a citizen,

00:03:38.943 --> 00:03:41.803
a queer subject, and much, much more.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.000
Now, in the Academy, people like me

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.220
aren't supposed to have favorites

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.070
or favorite books, but this book,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.830
and this scholar, the book at least

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.370
belongs on the shortlist, the small shelf

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.380
I think of most important books

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.840
published on these subjects
and on other subjects,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.740
it's one that's deserving
of all of our attention,

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.510
and many more people's attention

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.020
and to Ana's great
credit, she's written this

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.060
in engaging prose and lively prose

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.610
in ways that should
engage a popular audience

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.840
and deserve to find their
way to a popular audience.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.330
So please join me on welcoming,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.474
Ana Minian back to campus.

00:04:16.474 --> 00:04:19.474
(audience applauds)

00:04:24.403 --> 00:04:26.450
- Well, with that, I don't know

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.700
what else to say, I was blushing,

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.320
it was the nicest introduction

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.513
I've ever received.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.930
It is a real honor to be
here to present my work

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.930
with the Center for the
Study of Race, Indigeneity,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.650
and Transnational Migration,
when I was at Yale,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.750
this Center did not exist
and it's really incredible

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.790
that it is here now.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.860
I want to especially thank, Nandi Cummings

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.253
and Steve Pitti for organizing my visit.

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.630
This book started as Steve was saying,

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:02.980
as a dissertation, here at Yale,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.620
and it might be the most exciting moment

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.240
of my career to come back in order

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.183
to talk about it as a book now, here.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.540
Approximately 40% of all Mexicans

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.770
from the state of Zacatecas now reside

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.070
in the United States.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.840
Similarly, one third of all of those

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.970
from the state of Guanajuato now live

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:35.400
north of the border, and slightly more

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.550
Michoacanos, those from
the state of Michoacan

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.980
now work in the fields of California,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.120
Oregon and Illinois, then the number of

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.630
Michoacanos who still
reside in their home state.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.510
Latinos have become the largest

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.470
minority group in the United States,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:58.960
constituting approximately
18% of the population.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.580
There are an estimated 11 million

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.160
undocumented migrants, half of them

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.770
from Mexico now residing here.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.190
The sheer number of Mexicans who migrated

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.910
to the United States and their importance

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.950
in politics, economics, and daily

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.578
social relations means that their stories

00:06:19.578 --> 00:06:24.453
have shaped both Mexican
and US national histories.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.590
Yet few historians have paid attention

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:33.190
to the causes and effects
of Mexican migration

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.630
in Mexico, and even fewer, have examined

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.910
how this phenomenon affected life

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.150
in both countries, creating
strong transnational

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.560
relations between them, incorporating

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.599
these dimensions, changes
our understandings

00:06:51.599 --> 00:06:55.730
of both migration and of US/Mexico

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.633
relations more broadly.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.660
My book, Undocumented
Lives, the Untold Story

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:06.300
of Mexican Migration examines this history

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.210
in the post-war years, it does so

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.900
by using sources that come from

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.930
the National Archives of both countries.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:17.767
By using migrants personal collections

00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:20.670
which included sources
such as the love letters

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:24.100
that migrants sent to
their communities back home

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.280
and the records of their
meetings, and by using

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.420
over 250 oral history
interviews that I conducted

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.050
on both sides of the border, with people

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.111
who came from communities from the states

00:07:37.111 --> 00:07:39.451
of Michoacán, Zacatecas
which I pointed out

00:07:39.451 --> 00:07:41.638
at the beginning, which
were some of the states

00:07:41.638 --> 00:07:44.223
of highest out migration.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:47.740
Using these sources, I examine

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.411
how unauthorized Mexican migration

00:07:50.411 --> 00:07:54.000
became an entrenched phenomenon

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.000
in the years between 1965 and 1986.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:03.340
I used these two dates
as book ends to my book

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:05.940
because they mark important turning points

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.563
in the history of migration.

00:08:08.806 --> 00:08:13.650
Up until 1964, Mexico
and the United States

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.020
had a bilateral program through which

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.420
Mexican workers could
come as guest workers

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.403
and work legally for
short periods of time.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.460
In 1964, the United States unilaterally

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.431
ends this program, which was called

00:08:29.431 --> 00:08:32.360
the Bracero program, as a result,

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.470
the following year in
1965, Mexican workers

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.950
who were accustomed to coming here

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.750
and working, find themselves that they can

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.460
no longer come to the
United States legally.

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:48.150
But their conditions in
Mexico haven't changed

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.410
and so they continued coming, they simply

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:53.260
do so without papers.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:57.120
Still, their migration is,
for the most part, circular.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.200
Workers come, work here
for a short periods

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:04.800
of time, return to Mexico, and then repeat

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.848
this process, this pattern
of circular migration

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:13.630
ended in 1986, when the US Congress passed

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.700
the Immigration Reform and Control Act.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.200
One of the main clauses of this law

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.560
was border fortification, the Act allotted

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.680
a lot of money to increase the size

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:31.480
of the border patrol and
surveillance technologies

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:34.460
across the border, as it became harder

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.849
for people, for Mexicans to cross

00:09:37.849 --> 00:09:41.660
the border without papers, they decided

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:43.900
to come and settle permanently,

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.440
because they could no longer continue

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.570
to risk going back and forth now that

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:53.663
the border was so much harder to cross.

00:09:54.662 --> 00:09:59.310
The years between 1965 and 1986 were ones

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.560
in which many of the current dilemmas

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.293
around undocumented migration were born.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.740
This period saw the unprecedented rise

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.173
of unauthorized Mexican migration.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.980
Apprehensions by the Immigration

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.570
and Naturalization
Service, otherwise known

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:21.147
as the INS, rose from 55,000 in 1965

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.940
to over one and a half million by 1986.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.763
This is over a 3000% overall increase.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:33.910
This period also saw key shifts

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.920
in the government
policies of both countries

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.230
with regards to migration,
and perhaps more important,

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:44.720
it was during this period
that Mexican peoples

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.240
consciousness shifted
toward an acknowledgement

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.313
that their future depended
on undocumented migration.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.560
Because of time constraints,
I will not be able

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.870
to touch on every aspect that I talk about

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:04.570
in my book, instead, I
will focus on talking about

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.810
the broad arguments that I make

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.490
through the question of welfare.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.280
I do so because the question of welfare

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:17.650
alongside with ones of gender, sexuality

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:21.080
and geographical space, because welfare

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:23.440
is one of the main threads
that run throughout

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:27.230
the book, alongside those other threads.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:30.956
Now focusing on welfare
today, I will discuss

00:11:30.956 --> 00:11:35.150
two of the main points
that I make in my book.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.770
First, I argue that during the 1970s,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:43.210
working class men faced
exclusionary pressures

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.163
to leave both Mexico
and the United States.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.820
Now, that migrants are excluded

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:53.830
from the United States
seems, especially nowadays,

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.370
almost intuitive, but that Mexicans

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:02.320
were excluded from Mexico,
is much more surprising.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.840
It means that migrants
faced a double exclusion

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.343
from the two countries where they lived.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:12.400
This double exclusion was enacted both

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:14.277
by the government officials of Mexico

00:12:14.277 --> 00:12:17.230
and the United States as well

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.653
as by migrants own communities.

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:23.940
Let me explain this point a bit further.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:27.100
In Mexico, the government's
top policy makers

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.630
concluded that because the nation

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:34.630
could no longer afford
to act as a welfare state

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.660
because the Mexican economy
was starting to decline,

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:43.660
it had to find a way
to reduce unemployment

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.328
and one of the ways to
do so was to encourage

00:12:46.328 --> 00:12:50.320
people to emigrate, on the other side

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:52.900
of the border, in the United States,

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.700
migrants started to find themselves

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:57.899
accused of draining welfare and they were

00:12:57.899 --> 00:13:02.470
regularly deported,
migrants own communities

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:05.400
started to reject migrants presence

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:08.927
in either Mexico or the United States

00:13:08.927 --> 00:13:12.920
on a permanent basis, increasingly,

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.450
migrants found that they
could belong nowhere

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:21.399
that they could live on a permanent basis

00:13:21.399 --> 00:13:25.690
neither in the United
States, nor in Mexico.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:28.150
They started to claim that they were

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.630
from neither here nor there,
(speaks foreign language).

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:36.760
Now my second argument, is
that during this period,

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.430
migrants advanced their own visions

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.420
of where they belonged, by doing so,

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.780
they countered their double exclusion

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.470
from Mexico and the United States,

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.586
in particular, I focus on how they created

00:13:50.586 --> 00:13:53.530
groups in Los Angeles, that provided

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.900
economic security for migrants

00:13:55.900 --> 00:14:00.520
in the United States as
well as for Mexican citizens

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.090
who remained in Mexico.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:05.640
By guaranteeing that those back home

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.860
had their basic needs satisfied, I argued,

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:11.380
these migrant organizations started to act

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:15.550
as a type of extra-territorial
welfare state.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:17.830
Through this activism, migrants managed

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.610
to become central players in regional,

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:24.200
national and transnational politics.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.050
Now to make these two arguments today,

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.300
I will first show how the US government

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.580
then the Mexican government, and then

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:34.170
Mexican communities, used ideas of welfare

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.320
to deny migrants the right to settle

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.280
permanently in any place,
I will then move on

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:43.560
to show how by creating
welfare organizations,

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.480
migrants managed to create their own

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.423
cartographies of belonging.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:52.090
Let's start in the United States,

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:53.840
where one of the main rallying cries

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.520
against unauthorized Mexicans starting

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.470
in the 1970s, was that they were

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:02.810
draining the nation's welfare coffers.

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.350
During this decade of economic crisis,

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:08.070
the US economy saw the rapid rise

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:12.180
of unemployment and
inflation simultaneously.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.090
The expansion of welfare came to be seen

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.760
as an urgent national problem

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.680
and people of color were often accused

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:22.503
for draining welfare.

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:28.387
Newspapers often blame the "black,

00:15:28.387 --> 00:15:32.250
"promiscuous, Cadillac
driving welfare queen,"

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.900
for the expansion of welfare.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:38.582
This has led historians to focus strictly

00:15:38.582 --> 00:15:41.330
on the cultural associations between

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.260
black people and welfare abuse,

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.900
yet the majority of newspaper articles

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.920
that talked about the problems created

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.900
by Mexican migrants, also referred

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.970
to their use of public aid and services.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:59.088
Articles came out with titles such as,

00:15:59.088 --> 00:16:03.560
"illegal aliens here, called
public services burden,"

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:08.257
and "welfare system a
haven for illegal aliens."

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:13.700
Mexicans tried to
counter these stereotypes

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.630
by blaming US-born Mexican-Americans

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:19.460
for the existing associations between

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:23.050
Mexicans and welfare abuse, for instance,

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.712
Agapito Rodriguez, who
migrated from Zacatecas

00:16:25.712 --> 00:16:28.880
held that everyone in
the migrant community

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:32.820
believed that it was US
born Mexican-Americans

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:36.570
who were abusing welfare,
among his friends,

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.534
he claimed, Mexican-Americans were seen,

00:16:39.534 --> 00:16:43.917
"as lazy people, we,
migrants, would go to work,

00:16:43.917 --> 00:16:46.237
"but those people generally did not work,

00:16:46.237 --> 00:16:48.727
"but instead survived from welfare."

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.540
This commonly used
strategy of shunning other

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:57.680
marginalized groups did not
help undocumented migrants

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:01.090
who continued to be
associated with abusing

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:05.900
public welfare, these
associations were particularly

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:09.070
unsuitable because unauthorized migrants

00:17:09.070 --> 00:17:13.040
were ineligible for public
welfare and few dared

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.370
to apply for aid legally,
at least six different

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:19.350
field studies published at the time

00:17:20.377 --> 00:17:22.510
found that unauthorized Mexican migrants

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:27.080
rarely used public support,
such as unemployment

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:30.743
compensation, welfare
assistance or food stamps.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.830
This meant that Mexican migrants

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:37.060
were simultaneously accused of draining

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.360
public resources and at the same time,

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.493
prevented from applying for welfare.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:47.850
Still, one of the main
arguments hurting Congress

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:51.350
against undocumented
migrants, was of course,

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:53.570
that they drained welfare.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:56.210
In 1976, those who wanted to limit

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.910
the number of Mexicans
in the United States,

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:01.520
had a clear victory with the passage

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:04.430
of the Western Hemisphere Act.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:07.180
This Act established an annual quota

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:09.930
of twenty thousand immigrants per nation

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:12.660
from countries in the Western Hemisphere,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.980
a clause that affected
Mexico in particular,

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:19.120
as it was the only country
with an immigration rate

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.810
much higher than twenty thousand per year.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.520
The passage of the bill
was defended in part

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.610
through arguments such as those of

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:30.540
Senator Thurmond of South Carolina,

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.747
who argued that, "in many cases,

00:18:33.747 --> 00:18:35.897
"these illegal entrants and their families

00:18:35.897 --> 00:18:39.077
"soon become a welfare
burden on our society.

00:18:39.077 --> 00:18:41.607
"Supported in one way or another

00:18:41.607 --> 00:18:45.520
"by American taxpayers," arguments

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:48.150
that Mexican migrants drained welfare

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.570
did not simply affect
the marking of the border

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.599
through legislative means, they also

00:18:54.599 --> 00:18:57.740
encouraged the INS to increase

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.970
the number of raids that it was conducting

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:03.010
in Latino communities.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.730
In June 1973, Donald T. Williams,

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:10.600
then Acting Director of the INS, explained

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:12.280
the increasing number of raids,

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:15.267
by stating, "immigrants attend schools

00:19:15.267 --> 00:19:17.877
"at taxpayers expense, they take jobs

00:19:17.877 --> 00:19:20.147
"that normally would got to Americans,

00:19:20.147 --> 00:19:22.197
"and many of them go on welfare

00:19:22.197 --> 00:19:24.767
"and use other public social services."

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:29.780
So, notions of welfare,
played a significant role

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.600
in pushing migrants out
of the United States

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:35.370
by encouraging US officials to introduce

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.910
legislative changes to immigration law

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.180
and by encouraging them to conduct

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:43.270
more raids against undocumented workers.

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.790
Although Mexicans kept crossing the border

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.960
and living in the United States,

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.240
policy makers accused them of

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:53.253
being outsiders who drained welfare.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:57.220
Now let me turn to the case of Mexico.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:00.860
Like US officials, during the 1970s,

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:04.630
Mexican officials also started to reject

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:06.913
migrants presence in their country.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.610
The Mexican government
had a very different

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:14.650
rhetoric on welfare than
did US policy makers.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:17.930
After the Mexican
revolution, the ruling party,

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:21.060
The PRI, established
itself as the promoter

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:23.570
of the Mexican Welfare State.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:26.290
But even though the government
legitimatized itself

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:28.740
through claims that it
was a welfare state,

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:31.770
it failed to ensure the economic wellbeing

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:32.973
of its citizens.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:37.580
Up until the mid-1970s,
Mexican policy makers

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.100
had long considered that to achieve

00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:43.800
a welfare state, and
accelerate economic growth,

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:48.240
they had to pursue
population growth policies.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:51.030
In fact, since Mexico's independence,

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:53.270
Mexican officials had tried to increase

00:20:53.270 --> 00:20:56.210
the number of people who
resided in the country

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:59.770
in order to accelerate the country's

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:03.170
economic development, now
in terms of migration,

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:06.150
what this meant of course, was encouraging

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:07.880
those who had already
migrated, those who were

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.088
already in the United
States to return to Mexico

00:21:10.088 --> 00:21:13.260
while discouraging others who were

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.173
still in Mexico from going abroad.

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:20.570
In the mid-1970s, Mexico's top politicians

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:22.790
started to change their position.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:25.730
In part, because of
the collapsing economy.

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:28.220
During these years, the balance of payment

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:33.180
ran a huge deficit and
consumer prices doubled.

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:36.087
Before leaving office in 1976,

00:21:36.087 --> 00:21:38.370
President Luis Echeverría was forced

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:41.030
to devalue the Peso, ask for assistance

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:44.633
from the IMF, and accept
its austerity package.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.950
Mexican officials debated how to solve

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:50.440
the nation's economic problems.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:52.880
Should they continue to
expand the welfare system

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:55.570
even further, should they start to change

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:59.610
their position, ultimately, they concluded

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:03.630
that population growth was
no longer advantageous.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.300
If anything, Mexico's population

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:09.690
had to be reduced, one way to do so

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.300
was by accepting and sometimes even

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:15.600
by encouraging citizens emigration

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.143
to the United States.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:20.740
Now existing government archives

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:23.100
contain limited explicit evidence

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:26.130
in which policy makers openly acknowledge

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.410
changing their views on immigration

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:32.690
as a way of alleviating
Mexico's economic problems.

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:35.240
Speaking of such a
change, would have meant

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:38.290
recognizing that the PRI was giving up

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.780
on the revolution's welfare goals

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:43.340
of providing Mexicans with the means

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:46.280
of residing with humane living standards

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:48.440
in their country of birth.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:50.960
It would have also meant acknowledging

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.850
that the Government rejected the presence

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:57.600
of some citizens in the national territory

00:22:57.600 --> 00:23:01.700
and that officials had started to favor

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.500
their departure to a
country where they were

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:07.657
viewed primarily, as "illegal aliens."

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.608
Despite the paucity of
such explicit evidence,

00:23:11.608 --> 00:23:15.150
the jumbled changes in state practices

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:17.660
that occurred between the mid-1960s

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:21.180
to the mid-1980s, elucidate politicians

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:23.860
changing positions, let me give you

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:25.780
a couple of examples that illustrate

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.210
how they went from discouraging

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:31.690
unauthorized migration to
viewing it as necessary.

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:35.820
First, from the mid-1940s
to the mid-1970s,

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:38.380
Mexican and US migratory officials

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:41.520
had coordinated mechanisms to repatriate

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:44.940
deportees deep into
the interior of Mexico.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:47.426
These officials knew that if they released

00:23:47.426 --> 00:23:50.710
the deportees near the border region,

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:52.940
these migrants would simply cross over

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:55.963
to Mexico the following day.

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.820
So the mechanism they introduced,

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:01.940
worked like this, when
immigration officials

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:04.920
in the United States caught
unauthorized migrants,

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.690
they would take them
to the US/Mexico border

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:10.850
and they would hand them
to Mexican officials

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:14.260
from the Mexican Department of Migration.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.870
This Department of
Migration was then in charge

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:19.220
of taking the migrants
away from the border

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:22.080
and deep into the Mexican interior.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:25.130
And here we can see,
officials from Mexico's

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:27.610
Departamento de Migración,
Department of Migration,

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:29.890
and a group of men that
has been apprehended

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:32.450
and is about to board on a train

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.183
to take them into the interior of Mexico.

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:41.970
Now, in the mid 1970's
when Mexican officials

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:44.830
started to look favorably on immigration,

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.560
they stopped attempting to take deportees

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:51.870
away from the border and
into the Mexican interior.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:54.453
This change began in 1976.

00:24:55.310 --> 00:24:57.200
When US officials wanted to sign

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:01.360
a two million dollar contract
with Mexican officials

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:05.670
to be able to send deportees by plane

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:08.730
into the Mexican interior,
the Mexican government

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:11.940
for the first time, refused
to give these companies

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:15.900
permission to work, the
government officials

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.312
claimed that the deportees had the

00:25:18.312 --> 00:25:22.400
"constitutional right" to
stay in Northern Mexico

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.988
if they so desired, now let me point

00:25:24.988 --> 00:25:27.790
that before this point,
those constitutional rights

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:28.993
didn't seem to matter.

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:34.150
Another example of
politicians changing position

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:37.620
is that by the mid-1970s,
they started to work

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:41.360
on defending the rights
of undocumented migrants

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.420
in the United States, such attempts

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:47.200
to protect Mexicans in the United States

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:49.960
are not mentioned in government documents

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:52.872
until after Mexico's top politicians

00:25:52.872 --> 00:25:57.400
changed their position
with regard to immigration.

00:25:57.400 --> 00:26:00.020
The migratory worker archives,

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:01.810
which are all the archives that document

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.663
the instances in which Consuls assisted

00:26:04.663 --> 00:26:07.780
Mexican workers who lived
in the United States

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:10.770
as well as Mexican National Archives,

00:26:10.770 --> 00:26:13.155
all indicate that the concern over

00:26:13.155 --> 00:26:16.930
undocumented migrants rights did not begin

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:21.130
until the late 1970s,
when Mexican officials

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:24.630
had started to view immigration favorably.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:27.600
On the contrary, existing
government documents

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:30.830
show how before the mid-1970s,

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:34.360
Mexican officials insisted that Mexicans

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:37.470
who migrated illegally, lost their right

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:39.970
to protection as a result of their

00:26:39.970 --> 00:26:44.360
unauthorized migration, this strategy,

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:47.160
which they communicated to Mexican workers

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:50.140
comes out in the
correspondence that they sent

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:52.730
to workers, such as in the letters

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:55.983
that government officials
sent to Epifanio Quintín.

00:26:57.080 --> 00:27:00.510
On September 30, 1968, Quintín's son

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:04.030
died from asphyxiation after his smugglers

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:07.273
left him in a hermetically
closed U-Haul truck.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.930
For Quintín's family, the tragedy

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:12.633
was compounded by their poverty.

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:15.060
As Epifanio Quintín explained to

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:17.400
the Mexican Consul in San Antonio.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:19.670
He could not support his wife

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:22.470
and his son's children with the five

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:26.137
daily pesos that he made
as an ice cream vendor.

00:27:26.137 --> 00:27:29.179
Quintín requested that
the government allow

00:27:29.179 --> 00:27:32.629
one of his other sons to
go to the United States

00:27:32.629 --> 00:27:33.623
as a bracero.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:37.390
Government officials
replied that they could not

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:40.270
allow Quintín's other sons to go

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:42.601
to the United States as braceros because

00:27:42.601 --> 00:27:44.601
the Bracero Program had officially ended

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:48.757
but they then added
that they "considered it

00:27:48.757 --> 00:27:53.377
"convenient to emphasize
that Quintín's sons

00:27:53.377 --> 00:27:57.157
"not abandon their residence
without the existence

00:27:57.157 --> 00:28:00.347
"of a legally authorized work contract,

00:28:00.347 --> 00:28:03.267
"as they would other expose themselves

00:28:03.267 --> 00:28:06.127
"to leaving the country
without the protection

00:28:06.127 --> 00:28:08.487
"of the law and would run the risks

00:28:08.487 --> 00:28:12.640
"to which those who break
such laws are exposed."

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:16.230
Before the mid-1970s, officials insisted

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:19.490
that migrants lost their
right to protection

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:23.080
as a result of their
unauthorized migration.

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:25.890
In contrast, by the mid-1970s,

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:29.010
government officials
repeatedly informed migrants

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:31.130
of their aims to defend them.

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:33.620
In 1978, for instance, the government

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:37.988
publicly announced the
opening of the first office

00:28:37.988 --> 00:28:39.890
for the protection of
undocumented workers,

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:41.763
living in the United States.

00:28:42.785 --> 00:28:46.300
This new interest in the
human rights of Mexicans

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.590
who resided in the United States,

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:51.941
is particularly surprising
given the government's

00:28:51.941 --> 00:28:56.150
blatant disregard for
the rights of citizens

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:59.320
who still resided in Mexico.

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:02.997
As Human Rights Watch noted,
"since the late 1960s,

00:29:02.997 --> 00:29:06.937
"and early 1970s, an array of abuses

00:29:06.937 --> 00:29:09.257
"have become an institutionalized part

00:29:09.257 --> 00:29:12.647
"of Mexican society, including killings,

00:29:12.647 --> 00:29:17.310
"torture and abuses against
independent unions."

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.390
Officials interest in the human rights

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:23.340
of citizens in the United Sates,

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:26.330
and their concurrent
lack of concern for them

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:29.950
while in Mexico, was
related to their belief

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.950
that many Mexican citizens ought to live

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:34.063
north of the border.

00:29:35.833 --> 00:29:38.230
After Mexican officials
changed their views

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:41.670
regarding migration, they
also openly attempted

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.320
to convince the United States, to allow

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:47.050
undocumented migrants to reside there.

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:51.290
They even did so by
fanning out Cold War fears.

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:54.380
For example, in 1979, Mexico's Secretary

00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:56.930
of Foreign Relations, Jorge Castañeda,

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:58.240
asserted that the United States

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:00.800
was not giving Mexico
special consideration

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:02.885
when it came to immigration.

00:30:02.885 --> 00:30:05.140
Given that the United
States was not giving

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:07.020
Mexico the special consideration,

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:09.460
Castañeda said Mexico would start looking

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:13.767
to Latin America as well as to, "Europe,

00:30:13.767 --> 00:30:18.210
"Japan, The Socialist countries."

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:20.530
in this not-so-veiled threat,

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:23.170
he impressed upon the United States

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.900
how important immigration was

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.453
for Mexico's government.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:30.710
Before I continue, up to now

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:33.720
I have been arguing that during the 1970s

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:35.720
ideas of welfare pushed forward

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:38.060
the view that Mexicans did not belong

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:40.690
in the United States, these notions

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:43.000
encouraged US Government officials

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:46.161
to deport more Mexicans and made it harder

00:30:46.161 --> 00:30:50.100
for Mexican migrants to
enter the United States.

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:53.657
During this same decade,
Mexican authorities themselves

00:30:53.657 --> 00:30:57.170
started to reject the presence of migrants

00:30:57.170 --> 00:31:00.090
in Mexico, while the Mexican government

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:03.510
had previously attempted
to act as a welfare state

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:06.530
and continued to portray itself as such,

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:09.540
by the mid-1970s, Mexican officials

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:12.450
believed that the way
forward was to encourage

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:15.950
working class citizens
to live in a country

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:18.147
that viewed them as "illegals."

00:31:19.247 --> 00:31:21.440
Now that I have examined how migrants

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:23.927
experienced a double exclusion from Mexico

00:31:23.927 --> 00:31:26.920
and the United States,
because of the pressures

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:30.140
enacted by both governments, let me turn

00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:32.030
to the pressures migrants faced

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:34.370
from their own communities.

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:37.140
At the local level, migrants families

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:40.210
and friends also started to deny them

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:42.650
the ability to reside permanently

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:44.003
in either country.

00:31:45.140 --> 00:31:47.540
Because the Mexican
government failed to act

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:49.820
as a true welfare state on which people

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:53.060
could rely in time of need, families

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:56.220
and communities started to pressure

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:59.803
married men to head north to support them.

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:02.680
Antonio Córdova, from
the town of Tzintzuntzan

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:06.710
Michoacán, recalls that
before he started migrating

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:09.370
he went to meet with government officials,

00:32:09.370 --> 00:32:12.320
to ask them for credit to buy oxen.

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:14.630
According to Córdova, if he had received

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:18.420
this credit and had been able to buy oxen,

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:21.080
he would have been able
to avoid going north,

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:24.608
which he did not want
to do, but he recalls

00:32:24.608 --> 00:32:27.660
that, these officials just said go,

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:31.300
I'll examine your case
on this date or that date

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:33.510
but they never did anything.

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:36.540
Without prospects for
jobs and nowhere to turn

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:40.660
for help, Córdova knew that
he would have to migrate.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:43.570
His own family members insisted

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:47.513
that he go to the United
States to support them.

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:50.090
Now, from this example, you might have

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:53.940
already picked up that
unauthorized migration

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.500
and the pressures people felt to migrate

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:59.460
had a profound gender dimension.

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:02.190
These gender dynamics often had

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:05.742
unexpected affects, in
Mexican communities,

00:33:05.742 --> 00:33:09.400
Mexican men bore the pressures to migrate

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.450
to support their families.

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:14.315
Although some women also migrated

00:33:14.315 --> 00:33:17.287
to work, during the years between 1965

00:33:17.287 --> 00:33:20.520
and 1986, it was mostly men who migrated

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:23.380
and the community pressure to do so

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:26.100
fell almost strictly on them.

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:28.660
After all, it was extremely expensive

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:31.320
for whole families to go north.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:34.010
In part, because of smugglers fees.

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.500
Divisions of labor within the family

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:38.990
led people to believe that men are

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:41.860
to go north for work in the United States

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:43.840
while women remained at home,

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:45.820
taking care of the children.

00:33:45.820 --> 00:33:48.756
As Maria Reyes from
Nochistlán, Zacatecas recalls.

00:33:48.756 --> 00:33:52.317
"I thought about migrating, but I couldn't

00:33:52.317 --> 00:33:54.027
"because of my many children.

00:33:54.027 --> 00:33:56.217
"I was tired of being
alone while my husband

00:33:56.217 --> 00:33:58.117
"was in the United States.

00:33:58.117 --> 00:34:00.077
"I would tell him, take me with you

00:34:00.077 --> 00:34:03.117
"and he would say, how
can I take you with me.

00:34:03.117 --> 00:34:06.527
"First, where would we
stay, we're too many.

00:34:06.527 --> 00:34:08.467
"Second, how can I take you, when it's

00:34:08.467 --> 00:34:13.245
"so hard to cross over," like most women,

00:34:13.245 --> 00:34:15.820
Reyes never considered asking her husband

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:19.400
to look after their
children while she migrated

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:21.673
and remitted money for the family.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:25.220
Women were also discouraged from migrating

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:27.830
because of sexual violence at the border.

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:30.720
Horrifying stories of the dangers

00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:33.710
that women faced circulated throughout

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:37.010
Mexican towns, discouraging many women

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:39.053
from attempting their luck in El Norte.

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:44.330
For example, in the town of
Ruiz Cortinez, Michoacán,

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:47.400
women warned each other about the dangers

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:50.550
of going to the United
States by telling the story

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:54.450
of one particular villager, whose smuggler

00:34:54.450 --> 00:34:57.200
had sexually abused her,
and according to them,

00:34:57.200 --> 00:34:59.253
her life had since been ruined.

00:35:00.479 --> 00:35:02.660
While women were discouraged from heading

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:07.100
to the United States, men
felt pressured to do so.

00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:09.680
Significantly, it was
women, in their roles

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:12.840
as wives and mothers,
who were the main people

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:15.050
who pushed men to migrate.

00:35:15.050 --> 00:35:18.800
For instance, Eloisa Garza,
from Nochistlán, Zacatecas

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:22.440
recalls that like many women, she insisted

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:25.100
that her husband migrate to provide

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:28.537
for their family's welfare,
"because he had to go

00:35:28.537 --> 00:35:31.717
"to make money to support
us, he had to work

00:35:31.717 --> 00:35:33.877
"to be able to provide us with food,

00:35:33.877 --> 00:35:37.490
"because in Mexico, life is very hard."

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:39.237
Her husband's parents and friends

00:35:39.237 --> 00:35:41.223
also pushed him to go.

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:45.460
The staggering rates of male migration

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.800
left many men feeling inadequate

00:35:47.800 --> 00:35:49.910
if they did not go to United States

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:52.010
to provide for their families.

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:55.090
Migrating, to ensure
their family's welfare

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:58.760
became a way to establish one's manhood.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:02.120
Rafael Ruano recalls
that like many other men,

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:04.170
he migrated with three friends,

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:07.780
who pressured each other
into taking the journey.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:10.440
Had Ruano not migrated, he would have felt

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:12.782
cowardly, he says, and probably,

00:36:12.782 --> 00:36:14.553
would have been ridiculed.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.300
Community pressure changed the way

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:22.030
many men felt about the
space of their home town.

00:36:22.030 --> 00:36:24.850
In oral history interviews, they regularly

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:27.500
describe the pressures
they faced to head north

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:29.540
as if their home towns were shrinking

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:32.470
around them and squeezing them out.

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:35.837
As Ricardo Perez explained,
"everyone expected

00:36:35.837 --> 00:36:38.437
"me to leave and come back with money.

00:36:38.437 --> 00:36:40.647
"I felt like I couldn't remain in town

00:36:40.647 --> 00:36:43.630
"without going to the United States."

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:45.820
But once in the United States,

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:48.460
men felt that their loved
ones, the very people

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:50.230
who had pressured them to go north

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:53.370
in the first place, now prevented them

00:36:53.370 --> 00:36:56.600
from residing there permanently.

00:36:56.600 --> 00:36:59.100
Once men were abroad, their wives,

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.660
children and parents, regularly

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:04.119
pressured them to return home.

00:37:04.119 --> 00:37:05.880
Everado Camacho for instance, recalls

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:09.370
that his wife and daughter
constantly reproached him

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:11.210
for leaving them alone too much

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:13.120
during his stays abroad.

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:15.970
From the United States,
men would receive letters

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.030
from their families begging them

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:19.533
to return home soon.

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:23.200
Up to now, I have shown how men

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:25.970
came to see their communities
in the United States

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:29.620
as places where they could
only reside in temporarily.

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:32.580
And their hometowns as spaces that shrunk

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:35.590
around them, and squeezed them out.

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:37.680
It is important to note, however,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:41.600
that women also saw their
sense of space diminished

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:43.883
as a result of men's migration.

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:46.620
After going to the United States,

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:49.840
men started to fear that their wives,

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.570
who remained in Mexico,
would cheat on them

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.620
and so started to prohibit them

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:57.620
from socializing outside the home.

00:37:57.620 --> 00:37:59.970
According to Annabel Tran, who lived in

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:02.137
Santa Fe de la Laguna, Michoacán,

00:38:02.137 --> 00:38:04.897
"villagers often gossiped about women

00:38:04.897 --> 00:38:08.237
"who socialized outdoors, and they claimed

00:38:08.237 --> 00:38:09.937
"that they were being unfaithful

00:38:09.937 --> 00:38:12.690
"to their husbands," because rumors

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:15.570
of infidelities often reached El Norte,

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:17.930
women started to fear that if they went

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:19.760
outside of their homes too much

00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:21.670
and rumor spread that they were cheating

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:24.480
on their husbands, the men would stop

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:28.450
sending them money, as such,

00:38:28.450 --> 00:38:31.830
many women started to avoid going outside

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:33.163
as much as possible.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:37.740
Migration is often associated with a sense

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.740
of cosmopolitanism and an opening

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:43.240
of people's words, but when it came

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:46.180
to unauthorized Mexican migration,

00:38:46.180 --> 00:38:49.010
both men and women, saw
their sense of space

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:51.790
diminished in significant ways,

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:54.113
as a result of men's migration.

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:57.700
Let me summarize what I have been saying

00:38:57.700 --> 00:39:01.780
up till now about the
pressures migrants experienced

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:03.393
on both sides of the border.

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:06.730
Migrants faced a double exclusion

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:08.620
that prevented them from residing

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:11.820
in Mexico, and the United States.

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:15.280
This exclusion was enforced
by government officials

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:17.760
from both countries as well as

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:20.490
by their own families and friends.

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:22.850
Mexico's leaders used migrants

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:25.740
as economically superfluous and favored

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:28.500
their departures from Mexico.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:31.270
US officials, came to consider migrants

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:33.420
as draining welfare resources,

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:37.339
and regularly deported them
from the United States.

00:39:37.339 --> 00:39:41.750
Migrants own communities
rejected their presence

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:44.230
in either country by holding

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:47.240
that their own welfare depended on

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:51.110
migrants remittances and return visits.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:54.340
Now, in this context, migrants sought

00:39:54.340 --> 00:39:58.070
to exert their local,
regional, and national

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:02.293
belonging by creating their
own welfare organizations.

00:40:03.130 --> 00:40:05.420
To demonstrate this, I'm going to focus

00:40:05.420 --> 00:40:10.110
on the activism of Mexican
nationals in Los Angeles.

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:12.770
One of the main migrant
institutions that worked

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:15.320
to help other migrants during these years,

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:18.240
was the Comité de Beneficencia Mexicana,

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:20.060
often referred to in English, as

00:40:20.060 --> 00:40:21.673
the Mexican Welfare Committee.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:25.470
When one examines the
applications for relief

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:28.200
that the Comité received, a paradox

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:30.913
regarding welfare soon becomes clear.

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:35.700
As we have seen, US
anti-immigrant practices

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:38.026
were often influenced by the rhetoric

00:40:38.026 --> 00:40:40.363
that Mexicans abused welfare.

00:40:41.380 --> 00:40:44.570
But these exclusionary
practices themselves

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:48.050
were often responsible
for driving migrants

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.030
into seeking aid, for example,

00:40:51.030 --> 00:40:54.280
in 1970, immigration officials detained

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:58.110
24 year Ubaldo Rosas, to deport him back

00:40:58.110 --> 00:41:00.210
to Mazatlán, Sinaloa.

00:41:00.210 --> 00:41:02.920
On the train ride south, the officers

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:06.420
beat him up and
confiscated his belongings.

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:09.180
Fearing for his safety, Rosas jumped

00:41:09.180 --> 00:41:11.640
from the train, but he miscalculated

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:14.580
the distance and fell on the rails.

00:41:14.580 --> 00:41:18.130
The train severed both
legs and two fingers

00:41:18.130 --> 00:41:21.920
from his left hand, Rosas came to require

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:25.510
a wheelchair, he couldn't afford

00:41:25.510 --> 00:41:30.260
to buy it himself, so he solicited aid.

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:33.370
But not from US state resources,

00:41:33.370 --> 00:41:36.233
but from the Comité de
Beneficencia Mexicana.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:40.180
Border enforcement led Rosas to seek

00:41:40.180 --> 00:41:43.220
welfare aid, yet rather than resorting

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:46.410
to US state resources, Rosas depended

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:48.760
on the support of other migrants.

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:52.140
The Comité provided him
with the financial support

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:53.643
and wheelchair he needed.

00:41:54.870 --> 00:41:57.140
The Comité became so influential

00:41:57.140 --> 00:42:01.250
that Mexican consuls and even the LAPD

00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:04.080
sent Mexican nationals to seek aid

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:07.350
from this organization, in other words,

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:09.690
migrants became central players

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:11.620
in providing each other with their

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:15.403
basic necessities in the United States.

00:42:16.716 --> 00:42:19.150
The Comité raised money primarily by

00:42:19.150 --> 00:42:20.810
renting out a building it owned

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:23.602
called The Casa del Mexicano,

00:42:23.602 --> 00:42:24.882
and this is a picture of how

00:42:24.882 --> 00:42:26.910
The Casa del Mexicano looked.

00:42:26.910 --> 00:42:28.930
By the mid 1970s, the main groups

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:32.260
that rented out Le Casa,
were clubes sociales,

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.470
often known by their contemporary term

00:42:34.470 --> 00:42:37.109
of hometown associations.

00:42:37.109 --> 00:42:39.460
These clubs worked to send funds

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:41.510
across the border to develop

00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:43.173
their communities in Mexico.

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:46.550
As a result they acted very differently

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:48.860
from the mutual aid societies

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:51.230
that most historians have analyzed,

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:53.620
which worked to support migrants

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:55.463
within the United States.

00:42:56.528 --> 00:42:59.130
Instead, clubes sociales, I argue,

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:03.030
acted as a type of
extraterritorial welfare state.

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:05.820
From the United States, clubs took on

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.090
the role of the Mexican government

00:43:08.090 --> 00:43:12.210
and sought to ensure the
welfare of all Mexicans.

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:15.264
Activists themselves
have this interpretation.

00:43:15.264 --> 00:43:18.450
In his unpublished
memoirs, Raúl Villarreal

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.040
who was one of the main club activists,

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:24.957
recalls that history is,
"one of a simple dishwasher

00:43:24.957 --> 00:43:27.597
"from a Los Angeles
restaurant, who by working

00:43:27.597 --> 00:43:29.797
"in clubes sociales, ended up helping

00:43:29.797 --> 00:43:33.407
"the people of Mexico more
than the government itself."

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:37.820
These groups started as
very small organizations.

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:40.924
In 1962, a group of migrants who

00:43:40.924 --> 00:43:43.700
was living in Los Angeles but who came

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:46.910
from the small Mexico rancho
of Guadalupe Victoria,

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:49.390
which is located in
the state of Zacatecas,

00:43:49.390 --> 00:43:51.620
decided to start raising funds

00:43:52.502 --> 00:43:54.290
to send money back home, and here you have

00:43:54.290 --> 00:43:58.000
a list of the first members
of this organization.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:43:59.630
As you can see, it's not very big,

00:43:59.630 --> 00:44:00.850
there's very few members,

00:44:00.850 --> 00:44:03.990
they're each donating a
dollar each at a time.

00:44:03.990 --> 00:44:07.200
This was a very small scale operation

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:09.930
to begin with, however, migrants

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:11.710
from other communities in Mexico

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:14.650
soon followed the example
of the Clubes Social

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:17.642
Guadalupe Victoria, and by the mid-1970s,

00:44:17.642 --> 00:44:21.300
a large community developed in Los Angeles

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:24.840
around the idea of sending aid to Mexico.

00:44:24.840 --> 00:44:26.490
The club culture that took shape

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.200
in Le Casa del Mexicano, revolved around

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:32.100
dances that happened every weekend

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:36.770
and which had approximately 350 attendees.

00:44:36.770 --> 00:44:41.230
Parties began at 8PM and ended at 2AM.

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:43.740
As they roared on, people would laugh,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:47.600
dance and converse until the music ended.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:50.620
Once the party's finished, migrants left

00:44:50.620 --> 00:44:54.210
with a renewed sense of
community and belonging.

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:56.467
The hosting club, with a profit

00:44:56.467 --> 00:44:59.628
of approximately $600.

00:44:59.628 --> 00:45:02.110
Club members sent the money they collected

00:45:02.110 --> 00:45:05.110
to local agents in
Mexico who were in charge

00:45:05.110 --> 00:45:07.760
of managing the funds, for example,

00:45:07.760 --> 00:45:10.210
the Clubes Social de Guadalupe Victoria,

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:12.620
remitted the money migrants fund raised

00:45:12.620 --> 00:45:15.670
to club agents residing in the rancho.

00:45:15.670 --> 00:45:17.520
These agents then distributed it

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:19.833
to the elderly on a weekly basis.

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:23.540
As the club developed,
it started to take on

00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:25.940
bigger and bigger projects.

00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:30.870
By 1987, the club had
also paved the ranchos

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:35.750
main street, fenced its school, set up

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:39.398
electric power lines, wait,
brought portable water

00:45:39.398 --> 00:45:43.120
and built a clinic, and this is a picture

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:45.013
of how the clinic looks today.

00:45:45.980 --> 00:45:48.710
In others words, migrants
in the United States

00:45:48.710 --> 00:45:52.200
raised money and club
representatives in Mexico

00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:55.470
ensured that it was
appropriately distributed.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.560
Together, they formed a welfare system

00:45:58.560 --> 00:46:00.840
that guaranteed that the ranchos'

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:02.853
basic necessities were met.

00:46:04.313 --> 00:46:07.610
Migrants felt their families and friends

00:46:07.610 --> 00:46:10.140
prevented them from settling in either

00:46:10.140 --> 00:46:13.890
their hometowns or in
their new communities,

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:16.260
but through their club activism,

00:46:16.260 --> 00:46:19.690
migrants managed to become central figures

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:21.840
in both these locales.

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:24.620
From the United States, migrants changed

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:27.220
the built environment
and living conditions

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:30.820
in Mexico, when people
walked through paved streets,

00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:33.920
attended clinics, or even flipped on

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:35.850
and turned their light switches,

00:46:35.850 --> 00:46:39.910
they had to think about
migrants' contributions.

00:46:39.910 --> 00:46:41.960
The very meaning of hometown

00:46:41.960 --> 00:46:44.870
became imbued with migration.

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:48.170
Similarly, by creating long-lasting spaces

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:50.780
where they could gather
in the United States,

00:46:50.780 --> 00:46:53.190
migrants managed to build a community

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:56.420
north of the border,
despite the impermanence

00:46:56.420 --> 00:46:58.503
of their individual stays there.

00:46:59.757 --> 00:47:02.360
Club members realized
that they were taking on

00:47:02.360 --> 00:47:05.150
responsibilities that were traditionally

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:07.700
assumed by the state, but they argued

00:47:07.700 --> 00:47:10.300
that if they did not,
the Mexican government

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:14.037
would not either, as
Gregorio Casillas explained,

00:47:14.037 --> 00:47:16.727
"the government wasn't
interested, for example,

00:47:16.727 --> 00:47:19.217
"it's the government's
obligation to provide

00:47:19.217 --> 00:47:22.037
"what the community needs,
like electric lights,

00:47:22.037 --> 00:47:24.657
"clinics, schools, but unfortunately

00:47:24.657 --> 00:47:27.717
"they didn't do it, so
we had to intervene."

00:47:28.890 --> 00:47:32.180
From the 1960s to the mid-1970s,

00:47:32.180 --> 00:47:35.210
the Mexican government ignored clubs.

00:47:35.210 --> 00:47:40.040
Finally, in 1986, Zacatecas'
Governor Genaro Borrego,

00:47:40.040 --> 00:47:42.620
realized that given the huge impact

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:44.313
that migrants had on the state,

00:47:45.233 --> 00:47:48.210
he could not continue to
ignore club activities.

00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:51.100
That year, he agreed to develop a program

00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:54.010
by which the state government would match

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:56.550
every dollar that migrant organizations

00:47:56.550 --> 00:48:00.490
sent to Zacatecas for the
betterment of their communities.

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:02.810
This agreement marked the first time

00:48:02.810 --> 00:48:05.718
that migrants were granted
official recognition

00:48:05.718 --> 00:48:08.440
by a state government.

00:48:08.440 --> 00:48:11.650
In 1999, the Mexican Government began

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:14.550
the Programa Tres Por Uno, three for one,

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:17.910
by which Federal, Municipal,
and State governments

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:20.490
all give a dollar for every dollars

00:48:20.490 --> 00:48:23.083
club send for the
betterment of communities.

00:48:24.110 --> 00:48:27.430
In 1986, the year that
Borrego got involved

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:30.010
with club activities, the US Congress

00:48:30.010 --> 00:48:33.510
passed the Immigration
Reform and Control Act

00:48:33.510 --> 00:48:37.360
which further fortified
the US/Mexico border.

00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:39.911
By then, however, undocumented migration

00:48:39.911 --> 00:48:43.983
had already become a
self-perpetuating phenomemon.

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:47.110
Migrants such as those involved in clubs

00:48:47.110 --> 00:48:49.910
had learned that through their migration,

00:48:49.910 --> 00:48:52.710
they could at least acquire partial,

00:48:52.710 --> 00:48:55.340
national and local belonging in

00:48:55.340 --> 00:48:58.280
the multiple spaces in which they lived.

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:01.360
Crossing the border became
increasingly dangerous

00:49:01.360 --> 00:49:04.550
after 1986, men started refraining

00:49:04.550 --> 00:49:07.030
from circular migration and instead,

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:10.750
encouraged their whole families
to cross the border once

00:49:10.750 --> 00:49:14.370
and relocate permanently
in the United States.

00:49:14.370 --> 00:49:16.364
They did not dare head back to Mexico

00:49:16.364 --> 00:49:19.200
for fear that they would not be able

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.560
to cross back north again.

00:49:21.560 --> 00:49:24.370
Now, rather than feeling pushed

00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:27.920
out off all the places
in which they lived,

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:30.970
and having a sense of themselves

00:49:30.970 --> 00:49:33.540
as being from neither here nor there,

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:37.330
migrants now started to feel entrapped

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:40.520
in the United States,
which they came to refer to

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:43.163
as la jaula de oro, or the golden cage.

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:47.170
My talk has focused on how, in the years

00:49:47.170 --> 00:49:51.140
between 1965 and 1986, migrants faced

00:49:51.140 --> 00:49:54.820
a double exclusion from
Mexico and the United States,

00:49:54.820 --> 00:49:56.700
but also they managed to assert

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:59.100
their own cartographies of belonging

00:49:59.100 --> 00:50:00.980
through their activism.

00:50:00.980 --> 00:50:03.570
Among other issues, my book also examines

00:50:03.570 --> 00:50:08.230
how queer men were the only ones

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:10.730
who could refrain from migrating.

00:50:10.730 --> 00:50:12.990
It examines the other
types of transnational

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.576
activism, in which migrants engaged,

00:50:15.576 --> 00:50:18.920
and then analyzes the
broader economic developments

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:22.080
that shape the policies of both countries.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:25.030
As such, I offer a
transnational perspective

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:27.350
of the history of Mexican migration

00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:29.393
and the growth of Latino communities.

00:50:30.310 --> 00:50:33.200
As a conclusion to
today's talk, let me point

00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:34.960
some of the ways in which the history

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:37.350
I just recounted sheds light

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:40.550
on the present situation
facing undocumented

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:42.403
Mexicans in the United States.

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:46.670
A growing number of US
citizen are once again

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:50.460
resorting to 1970s
stereotypes that portray

00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:53.670
Mexicans as draining welfare and calling

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:55.680
for their deportation.

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:58.320
Mexican government officials continue

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:01.560
to view with favor, the departure

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:04.220
of Mexican citizens to the United States

00:51:04.220 --> 00:51:07.830
and even now, have not implemented systems

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:10.580
to incorporate those
who are being deported

00:51:10.580 --> 00:51:12.960
into Mexico's economy.

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:15.760
US politicians continue to ignore

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:19.100
how border fortification in fact increased

00:51:19.100 --> 00:51:22.320
the number of people residing
in the United States.

00:51:22.320 --> 00:51:25.540
While before 1986, it was primarily men

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:29.237
who migrated and they did
so in a circular fashion,

00:51:29.237 --> 00:51:33.080
after the border became fortified in 1986,

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:36.790
entire families migrated
and settled permanently

00:51:36.790 --> 00:51:38.660
north of the border.

00:51:38.660 --> 00:51:42.330
But this history shows how, by uniting

00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:44.550
migrants can improve their conditions

00:51:44.550 --> 00:51:47.760
and achieve local,
national, and transnational

00:51:47.760 --> 00:51:51.599
recognition and against all odds.

00:51:51.599 --> 00:51:55.230
After all, the years between the 1960s

00:51:55.230 --> 00:51:58.690
and the 1980s are ones which both Mexican

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:01.520
and US historians described as ones

00:52:01.520 --> 00:52:04.610
that saw a rapid political shift

00:52:04.610 --> 00:52:07.950
from the left to the right, a decline

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:11.500
in leftist activism, and the growing

00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:14.520
demonization of the welfare state.

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:17.570
Still, during these years, unauthorized

00:52:17.570 --> 00:52:21.490
Mexican workers managed
to create their own

00:52:21.490 --> 00:52:24.150
cross-border welfare institutions,

00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:27.990
and to fight for their
needs in both countries.

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:29.623
Thank you very much.

00:52:29.623 --> 00:52:32.623
(audience applauds)

00:52:37.640 --> 00:52:41.970
Thanks everyone, and so now I would love

00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:44.240
to take some questions,
thoughts about the talk,

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:45.740
about the book if you read it.

00:52:47.328 --> 00:52:49.078
- [Steve] I'm gonna close the door.

00:52:51.900 --> 00:52:53.710
- Yeah.
- [Man] Thank you very much

00:52:53.710 --> 00:52:55.260
for your presentation, it was wonderful.

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:57.660
I really look forward
to reading your book,

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.470
I've worked on Mexican history.

00:52:59.470 --> 00:53:00.483
I'm writing my dissertation right now

00:53:00.483 --> 00:53:02.870
about Cancun and there's a problem there

00:53:02.870 --> 00:53:04.440
with migration, bringing people

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:06.161
from other parts of Mexico,

00:53:06.161 --> 00:53:08.658
(coughing drowns out speaker)

00:53:08.658 --> 00:53:10.120
so I'm wondering, maybe you address this

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:12.110
in the book, but you could talk more about

00:53:12.110 --> 00:53:14.127
the Mexican dimensions of the story.

00:53:14.127 --> 00:53:17.520
Why is Zacatecas and
Michoacán in particular

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:19.620
what was going on there,
with their political

00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:21.830
economies during this century?

00:53:21.830 --> 00:53:24.035
- That was forcing people to migrate?

00:53:24.035 --> 00:53:27.839
So these were historically
areas of high out migration.

00:53:27.839 --> 00:53:32.120
Their agricultural
communities with high levels

00:53:32.120 --> 00:53:34.080
of drought, so whenever there's a drought,

00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:37.280
people have to migrate,
there's very little options.

00:53:37.280 --> 00:53:40.120
They're also communities where during this

00:53:40.120 --> 00:53:43.200
and starting in the 1940s,
the Mexican government

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:44.520
started to implement what's known as

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:47.329
the Green Revolution, where it introduced

00:53:47.329 --> 00:53:52.280
new technologies to modernize
Mexican agriculture,

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:54.170
such as the use of new fertilizers

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:57.980
or irrigation, but it
didn't introduce them

00:53:57.980 --> 00:54:01.350
in every single area, so
areas that were not modernized

00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:03.820
for example, now had to compete,

00:54:03.820 --> 00:54:05.670
with more modernized areas, which was

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:07.910
basically impossible, and not only that,

00:54:07.910 --> 00:54:09.990
but the areas that were being modernized

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:12.850
often needed fewer workers, so that also

00:54:12.850 --> 00:54:14.800
left workers without a job,

00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:16.320
which encouraged them to migrate,

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:18.840
especially because they
had also been doing that.

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:22.930
Alongside that, from 1942 to 1964,

00:54:22.930 --> 00:54:25.060
Mexico and the United States

00:54:25.060 --> 00:54:26.640
had this program that I talked about,

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:28.860
the Bracero Program, which was bilateral

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:30.810
guest worker program for
which Mexican workers

00:54:30.810 --> 00:54:32.870
could come and work for
short periods of time.

00:54:32.870 --> 00:54:35.230
Much of the recruiting for that program

00:54:35.230 --> 00:54:38.380
happened in those states, in
the central plateau region.

00:54:38.380 --> 00:54:42.010
Now, once you have people
starting to go to United States,

00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:44.330
a culture of migrationists develop.

00:54:44.330 --> 00:54:47.608
People already know how
the uNited States work,

00:54:47.608 --> 00:54:50.860
they learn some English,
they know where to go,

00:54:50.860 --> 00:54:53.130
it makes it much easier for them

00:54:53.130 --> 00:54:56.190
to continue going to the
United States afterwards,

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:59.260
so in some ways, that
helped to promote migration

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:00.390
in following years as well,

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:03.000
so for all these reasons, those states

00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:04.930
are the states that were seeing

00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:06.560
highest out migration in the years

00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:09.527
between 1965 and 1986 and earlier as well.

00:55:09.527 --> 00:55:13.310
And later, it starts to
diversify to other parts

00:55:13.310 --> 00:55:15.090
of the country, but what's interesting,

00:55:15.090 --> 00:55:16.380
is that most people are not thinking

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:18.410
about the south, which is a migration

00:55:18.410 --> 00:55:20.500
that starts later, but
they're always surprised

00:55:20.500 --> 00:55:22.360
that it's not people from Northern Mexico

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:23.547
that were migrating more.

00:55:23.547 --> 00:55:25.630
I mean part of the reason that happens

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:28.270
is because Mexico was investing much money

00:55:28.270 --> 00:55:30.810
in Northern Mexico in a way that it wasn't

00:55:30.810 --> 00:55:33.350
investing in the central plateau region,

00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:35.333
in this western states.

00:55:38.820 --> 00:55:39.963
Any other questions?

00:55:45.191 --> 00:55:46.625
Yeah.

00:55:46.625 --> 00:55:48.925
- [Lady] When the wives.

00:55:48.925 --> 00:55:51.373
- Can I give you the mic?
- Oh sure.

00:56:01.613 --> 00:56:03.334
- [Lady] When the husbands of the wives

00:56:03.334 --> 00:56:06.530
that left and the wives were getting

00:56:06.530 --> 00:56:08.710
or more that the husbands were getting

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:10.667
concerned about the behavior of the wives

00:56:10.667 --> 00:56:14.520
did this sort of intensify an already

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:17.380
misogynistic culture or

00:56:17.380 --> 00:56:19.843
how did that shift if any?

00:56:22.710 --> 00:56:24.680
- We often have these stereotypes

00:56:24.680 --> 00:56:27.130
about Mexican machismo and it's every easy

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:28.990
to reproduce them, and part of what

00:56:28.990 --> 00:56:30.836
I'm trying to do here is say,

00:56:30.836 --> 00:56:33.510
there's machismo in some
way, it's conditional

00:56:33.510 --> 00:56:35.670
to the structures that
occur, and migration

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:38.120
was one of the structures
that was occurring,

00:56:38.120 --> 00:56:40.631
so yes, of course, there were some men

00:56:40.631 --> 00:56:44.040
who were already telling
women not to go outside

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:46.520
and stuff, but their
fear, their insecurities

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:48.611
just increased so much,
they were like we're away,

00:56:48.611 --> 00:56:51.270
what are our wives
doing, let's control them

00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:52.870
in a way that beforehand, they didn't have

00:56:52.870 --> 00:56:54.570
so much insecurity, because
they were always around,

00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:56.580
they were seeing what was happening.

00:56:56.580 --> 00:56:58.800
So their insecurities definitely increased

00:56:58.800 --> 00:57:01.130
when they were in the United States,

00:57:01.130 --> 00:57:03.520
and so they started to increase

00:57:03.520 --> 00:57:06.270
their control over women,
now the way they did it

00:57:06.270 --> 00:57:07.840
was not just through them, it was through

00:57:07.840 --> 00:57:10.310
this whole social world that existed

00:57:10.310 --> 00:57:12.250
of women in particular, who controlled

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:14.090
each others' movements, they controlled it

00:57:14.090 --> 00:57:15.890
through gossip and they controlled it

00:57:15.890 --> 00:57:18.670
because women often lived with their

00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:20.790
parents-in-laws, so their parents-in-laws

00:57:20.790 --> 00:57:24.150
would not allow their daughters-in-laws

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:27.020
to go outside, so it was a very much

00:57:27.020 --> 00:57:27.870
controlling situation.

00:57:27.870 --> 00:57:30.410
When men lived in Mexico,
they were the ones

00:57:30.410 --> 00:57:31.950
in control, they didn't have to rely

00:57:31.950 --> 00:57:34.220
on their parents-in-law, and often,

00:57:34.220 --> 00:57:35.960
women experienced much more liberty

00:57:35.960 --> 00:57:39.200
they claimed before men started to migrate

00:57:39.200 --> 00:57:41.190
in such high numbers, and I actually think

00:57:41.190 --> 00:57:43.620
that what thinking about this does,

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:45.720
is it disrupts the notion that Mexicans

00:57:45.720 --> 00:57:49.530
are always machismo, and
show how the structures

00:57:49.530 --> 00:57:51.883
really affect gender constructs.

00:57:52.819 --> 00:57:55.652
- [Lady] Thank you for clarifying.

00:57:57.692 --> 00:57:58.525
- [Man] Thanks so much for the talk

00:57:58.525 --> 00:58:00.680
and I really enjoyed reading your book

00:58:00.680 --> 00:58:03.580
and was particularly interested
in the appendix section

00:58:03.580 --> 00:58:08.580
about doing oral history with queer folks

00:58:10.060 --> 00:58:11.290
and thinking about queer migration

00:58:11.290 --> 00:58:13.070
and I wonder if you could
talk a little bit more

00:58:13.070 --> 00:58:14.800
about that aspect of the project

00:58:14.800 --> 00:58:17.120
and how you approached
that, why that's of interest

00:58:17.120 --> 00:58:19.690
and how that fits into the
story that you're telling.

00:58:19.690 --> 00:58:23.370
- Yeah, so this project
started originally,

00:58:23.370 --> 00:58:25.253
part of what I was looking at, I was

00:58:25.253 --> 00:58:28.600
I was going around
Mexico and I noticed that

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:31.340
in many areas of Mexico,
there are a lot of areas

00:58:31.340 --> 00:58:34.890
that had a lot of women,
had a lot of elderly people

00:58:34.890 --> 00:58:37.700
a lot of men had already migrated,

00:58:37.700 --> 00:58:40.230
but there was a lot of queer men

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:41.850
and I was like why is there such

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:43.290
a high proportion of queer men?

00:58:43.290 --> 00:58:45.323
Why are queer men not migrating?

00:58:48.323 --> 00:58:49.700
So at first, it did not start as

00:58:49.700 --> 00:58:51.110
an oral history project per se,

00:58:51.110 --> 00:58:53.750
but simply as an observation and then

00:58:53.750 --> 00:58:56.530
as I went about it, first it was the idea

00:58:56.530 --> 00:58:59.261
of how do I go about meeting people?

00:58:59.261 --> 00:59:01.410
And it actually proved much easier

00:59:01.410 --> 00:59:03.053
than I expected it to.

00:59:05.170 --> 00:59:07.160
I went to churches, churches was one

00:59:07.160 --> 00:59:08.673
of the main places where I met people

00:59:08.673 --> 00:59:11.300
and although we think of Catholic Priests

00:59:11.300 --> 00:59:13.950
as being homophobic,
actually in these towns,

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:15.640
everyone was okay with who was gay,

00:59:15.640 --> 00:59:17.610
who was not, it'd be like, oh yeah,

00:59:17.610 --> 00:59:19.330
Juanito, Perdito, you know, and they would

00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:21.996
introduce me to the, and I was like,

00:59:21.996 --> 00:59:23.150
(speaks foreign language)

00:59:23.150 --> 00:59:26.170
if you wanna talk to me, and actually,

00:59:26.170 --> 00:59:27.700
they would talk to me
and they would be like

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:30.219
they were fine talking about it,

00:59:30.219 --> 00:59:34.319
and everyone else in town knew who was gay

00:59:34.319 --> 00:59:37.900
so it was actually, it facilitated it

00:59:37.900 --> 00:59:40.580
and in larger towns, where not everybody

00:59:40.580 --> 00:59:43.150
knows each other so much,
the people who did know

00:59:43.150 --> 00:59:46.140
everyone I found out, was taxi drivers.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.000
Taxi drivers because they circulate

00:59:48.000 --> 00:59:49.860
through town all the
time because they talk

00:59:49.860 --> 00:59:52.750
they know everybody, and so I would just

00:59:52.750 --> 00:59:54.580
hop on a cab and be like, and you know,

00:59:54.580 --> 00:59:59.580
sometimes I had to play oh,
I'm fairly gender queer looking

00:59:59.600 --> 01:00:03.490
and so I'd like play this,
oh, and so I'd arrive

01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:06.210
to a group of men and I'd be like, hi,

01:00:06.210 --> 01:00:08.947
I'm looking for gay men,
who of you could take me

01:00:08.947 --> 01:00:11.030
and it was a group of taxi stands,

01:00:11.030 --> 01:00:12.440
and I was like who could take me,

01:00:12.440 --> 01:00:14.050
and they were like oh, he's gay,

01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:15.260
oh no, he's gay, and I was like ha ha,

01:00:15.260 --> 01:00:18.440
like it was the funniest thing

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:20.210
and then finally someone was like,

01:00:20.210 --> 01:00:22.230
sure I'll drive you around
and I'll introduce you

01:00:22.230 --> 01:00:24.170
to people, so as long as I was willing

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:26.195
to take their little
jokes and laugh with them

01:00:26.195 --> 01:00:28.090
and in the end they introduced me

01:00:28.090 --> 01:00:31.360
and they were, it's not
like they were homophobic

01:00:31.360 --> 01:00:32.862
in the sense that they were laughing

01:00:32.862 --> 01:00:35.450
at being queer as if that
was something to laugh at

01:00:35.450 --> 01:00:37.050
but also they were really close friends

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:38.440
with the queer men and treated them

01:00:38.440 --> 01:00:42.440
with respect, so I would
just talk to people

01:00:42.440 --> 01:00:44.929
and I found it really strange how

01:00:44.929 --> 01:00:47.020
originally, I found it really strange

01:00:47.020 --> 01:00:48.480
how people were really open about it

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.820
and how priests were, later, I realized

01:00:50.820 --> 01:00:52.680
I started looking at more manuscripts

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:54.080
and more archival manuscripts

01:00:54.080 --> 01:00:55.900
and I realized that this had been part

01:00:55.900 --> 01:00:57.940
of these communities for a very long time,

01:00:57.940 --> 01:00:59.853
that this wasn't necessarily new.

01:01:02.640 --> 01:01:04.940
And that gay men actually had a lot

01:01:04.940 --> 01:01:07.420
of power in these
communities because as I say

01:01:07.420 --> 01:01:09.952
in the book, they were able to take jobs

01:01:09.952 --> 01:01:13.630
that were very highly paid, so women,

01:01:13.630 --> 01:01:16.251
for example, were
florists, but stereotypes

01:01:16.251 --> 01:01:18.920
would say that gay men
would be better florists

01:01:18.920 --> 01:01:20.890
so they made a lot more money,

01:01:20.890 --> 01:01:25.030
which placed them in very
economically privileged

01:01:25.030 --> 01:01:28.080
situations, which in turn, gave them

01:01:28.080 --> 01:01:29.780
a lot of power in their community.

01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:32.831
So that also gave them some respect.

01:01:32.831 --> 01:01:35.693
So people were willing to talk about it.

01:01:44.430 --> 01:01:45.690
- [Lady] Thank you also for this,

01:01:45.690 --> 01:01:48.520
this was absolutely fabulous.

01:01:48.520 --> 01:01:50.920
Sort of going along with Adam's question,

01:01:50.920 --> 01:01:52.740
it sounds like you had a really rich

01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:54.870
and diverse source space
and I would love to hear

01:01:54.870 --> 01:01:57.610
about the process of
going through everything

01:01:57.610 --> 01:02:01.690
you found and being able to craft this,

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:03.090
what other projects you think

01:02:03.090 --> 01:02:07.460
you could and will create
out of all this information

01:02:07.460 --> 01:02:10.973
and how you chose to
privilege what information.

01:02:11.840 --> 01:02:13.853
- Yeah, that's a really great question.

01:02:17.044 --> 01:02:20.090
So I was interested in two different

01:02:20.090 --> 01:02:22.040
sets of questions, one
is what was happening

01:02:22.040 --> 01:02:25.327
at the Government level in
Mexico and the United States

01:02:25.327 --> 01:02:27.273
and one was what was happening to people.

01:02:28.160 --> 01:02:30.670
That's how it first stated,
so it was really easy

01:02:30.670 --> 01:02:33.178
to separate the chapters, I was like,

01:02:33.178 --> 01:02:34.560
okay these are the archives
of the Mexican Government

01:02:34.560 --> 01:02:37.340
these are the archives,
and that's how the book

01:02:37.340 --> 01:02:39.980
is written and it comes at a cost.

01:02:39.980 --> 01:02:41.560
Another way that I could have written it

01:02:41.560 --> 01:02:43.810
is in a much more
integrated way of how people

01:02:43.810 --> 01:02:46.440
experienced government
policies, which I think

01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:48.040
might have been more productive,

01:02:49.667 --> 01:02:50.949
so it did come with a cost,
and that's another way

01:02:50.949 --> 01:02:52.599
in which I could have written it.

01:02:54.090 --> 01:02:56.920
Your other question, and oh and then,

01:02:56.920 --> 01:02:59.039
what I did was I started incorporating

01:02:59.039 --> 01:03:01.460
other dynamics that I
wanted, so for example,

01:03:01.460 --> 01:03:03.904
activists and so I have
a chapter on activism

01:03:03.904 --> 01:03:08.890
and often, things jumped
around, I think the stuff on

01:03:10.070 --> 01:03:11.780
La Casa del Mexicano was originally

01:03:11.780 --> 01:03:13.380
in chapter four, then
it came to chapter two

01:03:13.380 --> 01:03:15.280
then it returned to four
and now it's in two again

01:03:15.280 --> 01:03:17.500
so it really did change
and there's many ways

01:03:17.500 --> 01:03:20.320
to write it, but I was trying to form

01:03:20.320 --> 01:03:24.180
a narrative that was easy to understand

01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:26.812
for people that showed
all the complexities

01:03:26.812 --> 01:03:29.323
but that was also readable.

01:03:39.790 --> 01:03:42.030
- [Lady] Thank you Ana
for a fascinating talk

01:03:42.030 --> 01:03:44.483
and a very informed breaking book,

01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:48.900
and I really appreciate how you complicate

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:52.070
a history of Mexican migration by

01:03:52.070 --> 01:03:55.000
centering both the roles
of the Mexican government

01:03:55.000 --> 01:03:58.970
and the US government and I'm intrigued

01:03:58.970 --> 01:04:00.990
by your argument about how Mexicans

01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:04.040
are displaced from both
Mexico and the United States

01:04:04.040 --> 01:04:06.460
but also from the local communities

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:09.237
and your picture of la Casa del Mexicano,

01:04:10.240 --> 01:04:13.170
made me realize that also
while they're being displaced

01:04:13.170 --> 01:04:16.720
they find ways to create their own home

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:19.180
by titling it la Casa del Mexicano,

01:04:19.180 --> 01:04:22.070
the home of the Mexican in this country

01:04:22.070 --> 01:04:25.620
that isn't ours, so that's
mostly an observation

01:04:25.620 --> 01:04:28.500
so thank you for connecting those points

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:31.570
that have not always been in the forefront

01:04:31.570 --> 01:04:33.340
of how I understand Mexican migration,

01:04:33.340 --> 01:04:35.720
but my question is about the role of women

01:04:35.720 --> 01:04:38.940
who stayed in Mexico, so while men

01:04:38.940 --> 01:04:42.600
were the main ones who
emigrated and were sending

01:04:42.600 --> 01:04:45.490
money back, what was the
role of Mexican women

01:04:45.490 --> 01:04:48.480
did they have any say,
or any leadership roles

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:51.220
in organizing how the
Money sent was being used

01:04:51.220 --> 01:04:53.930
to maybe do the churches, or the clinics

01:04:53.930 --> 01:04:57.933
or other services or were
they not involved at all?

01:04:59.770 --> 01:05:04.010
- So, unfortunately, most women were not

01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:06.130
involved at all to begin with because

01:05:08.303 --> 01:05:10.860
a lot of the money that was being sent,

01:05:10.860 --> 01:05:12.900
in terms of remittances, was being sent

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:14.880
to parents and then the parents-in-law

01:05:14.880 --> 01:05:17.220
would distribute it to
the family accordingly

01:05:17.220 --> 01:05:19.960
so they weren't in charge of that money

01:05:19.960 --> 01:05:24.960
but what did happen
was that in Los Angeles

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:28.690
there is this beauty pageant
that is very very important

01:05:28.690 --> 01:05:30.860
Señorita Mexico de Los Angeles.

01:05:31.970 --> 01:05:34.810
It's a huge pageant, the person who wins

01:05:34.810 --> 01:05:36.543
has a lot of power, and one year,

01:05:36.543 --> 01:05:38.933
this one women, María Elena Serrano,

01:05:42.490 --> 01:05:45.230
who's this lady over there, won

01:05:47.643 --> 01:05:49.670
Señorita México de Los
Angeles, and she was also

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:52.660
extremely charismatic and the leaders

01:05:52.660 --> 01:05:56.790
of clubes sociales realized
that she could help them

01:05:56.790 --> 01:06:00.510
raise a lot of money, so
they made her president,

01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:02.150
not just of one club, but of

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:05.410
the whole federation of
clubs, and at that point

01:06:05.410 --> 01:06:08.460
she tried to change the position of women

01:06:08.460 --> 01:06:10.690
within the clubs, now it wasn't much

01:06:10.690 --> 01:06:13.770
because few women were
migrating, women were staying

01:06:13.770 --> 01:06:15.750
primarily in Mexico, but for those women

01:06:15.750 --> 01:06:18.270
she did change it for
a short period of time.

01:06:18.270 --> 01:06:20.010
She didn't have much
success, but for a short

01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:22.360
period of time, she did
incorporate more women.

01:06:24.150 --> 01:06:25.620
As I showed there, part of what clubs

01:06:25.620 --> 01:06:28.516
were also doing, for
example, was bringing people

01:06:28.516 --> 01:06:33.516
such as Ramirez, who's okay,
she's called La Valentina,

01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:41.340
she's like this women in
the Mexican revolution

01:06:41.340 --> 01:06:44.260
who is known for her gender queerness

01:06:44.260 --> 01:06:46.160
and they brought her and she came,

01:06:46.160 --> 01:06:48.620
dressed In male garb in her full

01:06:48.620 --> 01:06:52.133
so they were, in some
ways, working with queer

01:06:52.133 --> 01:06:54.900
gender presentations and
changing gender norms

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:57.070
in that way, but in terms of the money

01:06:57.070 --> 01:06:59.910
that was sent, it was men
and the agent in Mexico

01:06:59.910 --> 01:07:01.130
who were in charge of the money

01:07:01.130 --> 01:07:05.073
were primarily men, after 1986,

01:07:05.073 --> 01:07:08.440
women do start taking
some positions of power

01:07:08.440 --> 01:07:11.640
in local communities,
but what happens often

01:07:11.640 --> 01:07:16.640
is that men are in charge of retuning home

01:07:16.690 --> 01:07:19.632
to be, so for political positions,

01:07:19.632 --> 01:07:22.940
of the ejido for example,
which are the communal lands,

01:07:22.940 --> 01:07:25.890
who's going to rule the
ejido if all the men go?

01:07:25.890 --> 01:07:28.500
You'd expect it to be women, but actually,

01:07:28.500 --> 01:07:31.010
what starts to develop in many communities

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:33.600
is that they force a particular man

01:07:33.600 --> 01:07:36.630
to return to be in charge of working

01:07:36.630 --> 01:07:39.860
in the ejido, so women don't acquire

01:07:39.860 --> 01:07:41.840
a lot of political power
in many communities.

01:07:41.840 --> 01:07:44.510
In some they start acquiring more power

01:07:44.510 --> 01:07:46.727
particularly in the state Zacatecas.

01:07:48.030 --> 01:07:48.923
- [Lady] Thank you.

01:07:57.700 --> 01:08:00.040
- [Woman] Hi, can you discuss the role

01:08:00.040 --> 01:08:04.090
of children in your book and what impact

01:08:05.470 --> 01:08:08.610
moving or not moving and staying in Mexico

01:08:08.610 --> 01:08:13.300
had and the role of mixed
status families in your book.

01:08:13.300 --> 01:08:15.550
- Well, there were few
mixed status families

01:08:15.550 --> 01:08:17.590
because for the most part, children

01:08:17.590 --> 01:08:21.090
were staying in mexico with their mothers

01:08:21.090 --> 01:08:23.130
while the men were migrating,

01:08:23.130 --> 01:08:24.840
so mixed status in the sense of more like

01:08:24.840 --> 01:08:27.100
transnational, more like split.

01:08:27.100 --> 01:08:29.567
Now we think of split families
when someone's deported

01:08:29.567 --> 01:08:32.330
but back then the splitness primarily came

01:08:32.330 --> 01:08:33.820
because the men was migrating,

01:08:33.820 --> 01:08:35.780
so you talk to all these children

01:08:35.780 --> 01:08:38.160
who say I grew up without
a father, I saw him

01:08:38.160 --> 01:08:41.330
coming and going, but he wasn't there.

01:08:41.330 --> 01:08:44.030
For example, I talked
about this guy Camacho

01:08:44.030 --> 01:08:45.980
and he talked to his daughter Karen

01:08:45.980 --> 01:08:48.320
and she says, I really resented him.

01:08:48.320 --> 01:08:50.470
I didn't understand why he was going,

01:08:50.470 --> 01:08:52.700
she was just a kid and tons of kids

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:54.600
describe the same thing.

01:08:54.600 --> 01:08:57.690
Now, boys, describe
something very different

01:08:57.690 --> 01:09:00.700
which is that they grew up knowing

01:09:00.700 --> 01:09:03.840
that they would have to
come to the United States.

01:09:03.840 --> 01:09:06.610
Now after 1986, of course, children

01:09:06.610 --> 01:09:08.630
start growing up here,
children who would have

01:09:08.630 --> 01:09:10.770
stayed in Mexico are now being brought

01:09:10.770 --> 01:09:13.300
to the United States, so when we think

01:09:13.300 --> 01:09:16.110
about people like Dreamers, right,

01:09:16.110 --> 01:09:19.710
who are being sad, oh like the dreamers,

01:09:19.710 --> 01:09:22.200
actually without IRCA,
without the fortification

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:23.640
of the border, those children would have

01:09:23.640 --> 01:09:25.970
probably stayed in Mexico.

01:09:25.970 --> 01:09:28.530
And it's this law that made it harder

01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:30.560
for people to cross
the border and continue

01:09:30.560 --> 01:09:32.450
to engage in circular migration

01:09:32.450 --> 01:09:35.613
that led children to grow
up in the United States.

01:09:37.046 --> 01:09:39.110
- [Steve] A segue to the
question I wanna ask Ana,

01:09:39.110 --> 01:09:42.280
could you just give us a little sense

01:09:42.280 --> 01:09:44.861
of the material you offer regarding

01:09:44.861 --> 01:09:47.980
struggles for human rights on the US side

01:09:47.980 --> 01:09:49.503
of the border in places like Arizona

01:09:49.503 --> 01:09:51.590
and Texas, I'm thinking in particular

01:09:51.590 --> 01:09:55.700
about the work that you did
on the Plyler v. Doe case

01:09:55.700 --> 01:09:58.079
which does get to questions around kids

01:09:58.079 --> 01:10:00.483
and the status of kids, the rights of kids

01:10:00.483 --> 01:10:02.660
and the involvement of parents

01:10:02.660 --> 01:10:04.340
in those struggles in particular.

01:10:04.340 --> 01:10:05.710
But you also have some wonderful material

01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:08.210
in the book related to labor rights,

01:10:08.210 --> 01:10:10.210
for migrants in Arizona and I wonder

01:10:10.210 --> 01:10:11.570
as we think about these questions

01:10:11.570 --> 01:10:14.522
about wellbeing, if you
might just present us

01:10:14.522 --> 01:10:17.230
at least briefly, with some
of your findings there.

01:10:17.230 --> 01:10:19.660
- Yeah, so some children did migrate

01:10:20.760 --> 01:10:22.460
and part of the struggle
that started happening

01:10:22.460 --> 01:10:27.089
in Texas was that the Texas legislature

01:10:27.089 --> 01:10:31.310
decided that school districts in Texas

01:10:31.310 --> 01:10:34.297
could decide whether to
allow undocumented migrants

01:10:34.297 --> 01:10:37.110
whether to start charging
them or whether not

01:10:37.110 --> 01:10:38.490
to allow them at all, or whether just to

01:10:38.490 --> 01:10:40.530
continue welcoming them, so every district

01:10:40.530 --> 01:10:42.030
could do whatever they wanted.

01:10:44.450 --> 01:10:47.783
And there's one district in Texas, Tyler,

01:10:48.660 --> 01:10:53.550
where the principal
decided that he was going

01:10:53.550 --> 01:10:56.840
to start charging admission, $1000

01:10:56.840 --> 01:10:58.070
for every undocumented migrant.

01:10:58.070 --> 01:10:59.510
Now, the kids could not pay that,

01:10:59.510 --> 01:11:01.240
the parents could not pay that,

01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:05.480
so ultimately, a group of parents

01:11:05.480 --> 01:11:06.850
whose children were undocumented

01:11:06.850 --> 01:11:09.340
decided to get together,
they went for help

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:11.330
everywhere they could, they couldn't find

01:11:11.330 --> 01:11:13.610
a lot of help originally, finally,

01:11:13.610 --> 01:11:16.230
a church member sent them to MALDEF

01:11:16.230 --> 01:11:18.610
and lawyers from MALDEF,
which was a very important

01:11:18.610 --> 01:11:22.453
Mexican-American organization,
took on their case.

01:11:24.700 --> 01:11:26.820
And you know, they built a case

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:30.820
that ultimately was called Plyler V Doe,

01:11:30.820 --> 01:11:32.660
Doe because of course, the children

01:11:32.660 --> 01:11:34.896
couldn't use their name,
right, that would have been

01:11:34.896 --> 01:11:37.100
a form, it would have been very easy

01:11:37.100 --> 01:11:38.930
to retaliate against their families

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:40.280
in the small town of Tyler,

01:11:42.788 --> 01:11:44.457
and Plyler because that was the name

01:11:44.457 --> 01:11:45.753
of the Superintendent of the school.

01:11:47.500 --> 01:11:49.510
And what was determined,
and you know MALDEF

01:11:49.510 --> 01:11:52.770
put this very important legal brief

01:11:52.770 --> 01:11:55.770
and they finally made
it to the supreme Court

01:11:55.770 --> 01:11:56.883
and they won.

01:11:58.170 --> 01:12:01.300
And the judges said yes,
undocumented children

01:12:01.300 --> 01:12:04.230
can attend school, but they can only

01:12:04.230 --> 01:12:06.522
but the reason why they
were determining this

01:12:06.522 --> 01:12:11.522
is because it "wasn't
the fault of their own."

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:14.750
What this language does
is that it portrays

01:12:14.750 --> 01:12:16.740
undocumented children as innocent

01:12:16.740 --> 01:12:18.683
and undocumented adults as guilty,

01:12:18.683 --> 01:12:21.376
without realizing the complex reasons

01:12:21.376 --> 01:12:24.700
why undocumented adults are coming.

01:12:24.700 --> 01:12:26.590
Now we don't have to fight for children

01:12:26.590 --> 01:12:28.540
to go to school, we still
have to fight for them

01:12:28.540 --> 01:12:32.607
to get into college for
free, to get the same amount

01:12:32.607 --> 01:12:34.760
of grants and benefits to be able

01:12:34.760 --> 01:12:37.180
to attend college, but we
don't have to fight that

01:12:37.180 --> 01:12:39.260
for public schools and we
don't have to fight that

01:12:39.260 --> 01:12:41.860
because of Plyler V Doe, but part of what

01:12:41.860 --> 01:12:44.210
this law also does is that it criminalized

01:12:44.210 --> 01:12:47.682
that it encouraged the
language that criminalizes

01:12:47.682 --> 01:12:52.313
unauthorized adults, but this was

01:12:52.313 --> 01:12:53.520
a very important struggle.

01:12:53.520 --> 01:12:55.060
There were other legal struggles

01:12:55.060 --> 01:12:57.900
that came about, one
of the ones that Steve

01:12:57.900 --> 01:13:01.780
was mentioning was in Arizona.

01:13:01.780 --> 01:13:03.640
At the time, most migrants were not going

01:13:03.640 --> 01:13:06.150
to Arizona, they were going to California.

01:13:06.150 --> 01:13:08.081
70% of all migrants were going to Cal,

01:13:08.081 --> 01:13:11.880
I'm from Stanford, so I
always say they're coming here

01:13:11.880 --> 01:13:13.310
they coming to California, they're going

01:13:13.310 --> 01:13:15.810
to California, but some were going

01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:19.110
to Arizona, especially from states

01:13:20.310 --> 01:13:22.360
in Mexico with lower out migrations

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:23.560
so one of the most important states

01:13:23.560 --> 01:13:25.639
where they were going to Arizona

01:13:25.639 --> 01:13:27.659
was the Guerrero, which
at the time didn't have

01:13:27.659 --> 01:13:31.010
such a high rate of migration.

01:13:31.010 --> 01:13:32.950
But every year, every season,

01:13:32.950 --> 01:13:35.980
seasonal workers would come, go to Arizona

01:13:35.980 --> 01:13:39.535
and work there, now, working in Arizona

01:13:39.535 --> 01:13:41.730
the jobs that, there were two jobs

01:13:41.730 --> 01:13:43.790
that existed for farm workers;

01:13:43.790 --> 01:13:45.490
working in the onion fields,

01:13:45.490 --> 01:13:48.430
and working in the groves
in the citrus fields.

01:13:48.430 --> 01:13:50.450
The onions paid much better, and it was

01:13:50.450 --> 01:13:52.640
much easier work, but
undocumented migrants

01:13:52.640 --> 01:13:56.600
could not do it, partially,
because immigration officials

01:13:56.600 --> 01:13:59.960
were always circulating
throughout Arizona,

01:13:59.960 --> 01:14:02.910
throughout this area, near Phoenix,

01:14:02.910 --> 01:14:05.380
and they would see undocumented migrants

01:14:05.380 --> 01:14:07.890
in the onion fields, but the groves

01:14:07.890 --> 01:14:09.920
had so much foliage that they could hide

01:14:09.920 --> 01:14:12.400
within them, so undocumented migrants

01:14:12.400 --> 01:14:16.330
lived in the groves, they
never left the groves

01:14:16.330 --> 01:14:17.640
they always lived there, they lived

01:14:17.640 --> 01:14:20.940
in cardboard boxes or on top of branches

01:14:20.940 --> 01:14:23.450
when there were sneaks to avoid them,

01:14:23.450 --> 01:14:27.010
they drank water that had fertilizer

01:14:27.010 --> 01:14:29.060
because that was what was available,

01:14:29.060 --> 01:14:30.530
the living and working conditions

01:14:30.530 --> 01:14:32.840
were absolutely dreadful.

01:14:32.840 --> 01:14:35.080
So one undocumented migrant decided

01:14:35.080 --> 01:14:36.930
that he wanted to organize these workers,

01:14:36.930 --> 01:14:39.310
he had Joined the United Farm Workers,

01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:41.450
the United Farm Workers at the time

01:14:41.450 --> 01:14:43.997
did not support the United Farm Workers

01:14:43.997 --> 01:14:46.600
are César Chávez's
movement, which at the time

01:14:46.600 --> 01:14:49.900
was not, this is 1977, at the time

01:14:49.900 --> 01:14:52.250
it was very conflicted
about whether to support

01:14:52.250 --> 01:14:53.610
undocumented immigrants or not,

01:14:53.610 --> 01:14:55.540
before then it had been very much opposed

01:14:55.540 --> 01:14:58.860
to undocumented migrants, so there's one

01:14:58.860 --> 01:15:01.490
organizer, Lupe Sanchez, splits away

01:15:01.490 --> 01:15:04.300
from the United Farm Workers, and decides

01:15:04.300 --> 01:15:06.910
that he is going to organize these workers

01:15:06.910 --> 01:15:09.755
in Arizona, in the citrus groves

01:15:09.755 --> 01:15:12.650
and the way he does it, is he realizes

01:15:12.650 --> 01:15:13.880
he can't go into the groves, these

01:15:13.880 --> 01:15:15.580
are private properties, the workers

01:15:15.580 --> 01:15:18.310
aren't coming out, so
what is he going to do?

01:15:18.310 --> 01:15:20.720
So what he does is he goes to Mexico

01:15:20.720 --> 01:15:24.290
and organizes workers in Mexico itself

01:15:24.290 --> 01:15:27.200
and he can do so because every season

01:15:27.200 --> 01:15:29.650
people from the same communities in Mexico

01:15:29.650 --> 01:15:31.790
would come to those fields in Arizona,

01:15:31.790 --> 01:15:33.630
so circular migration is a way

01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:35.700
which connects two very different places

01:15:35.700 --> 01:15:38.250
and allows them to organize a population

01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:41.250
that otherwise would
have been unorganizable.

01:15:41.250 --> 01:15:43.600
And not only that, but by being in Mexico,

01:15:43.600 --> 01:15:45.930
you were asking about
women, he meets their wives,

01:15:45.930 --> 01:15:47.670
he meets the priests, the priests talks

01:15:47.670 --> 01:15:49.720
to the men as well,
everyone convinces the men

01:15:49.720 --> 01:15:52.530
that it's indispensable
that they go and strike

01:15:52.530 --> 01:15:54.500
the following harvest season.

01:15:54.500 --> 01:15:56.250
This is really hard for them

01:15:56.250 --> 01:15:59.010
because the union that
Lupe Sanchez organized

01:15:59.010 --> 01:16:01.420
did not have any strike funds, so the men

01:16:01.420 --> 01:16:04.830
decide to go and strike, decide
to come across the border

01:16:04.830 --> 01:16:08.414
and strike even without having funds.

01:16:08.414 --> 01:16:12.610
The grove which they struck at was called

01:16:12.610 --> 01:16:15.650
Arrowhead Ranch, which
was particularly owned

01:16:15.650 --> 01:16:17.940
by the Goldwater family, so it received

01:16:17.940 --> 01:16:21.200
a lot of media attention
and it was the first

01:16:21.200 --> 01:16:24.150
successful strike by undocumented workers.

01:16:24.150 --> 01:16:25.780
So when we're thinking
about what all these

01:16:25.780 --> 01:16:26.930
what you were asking me, Steve,

01:16:26.930 --> 01:16:29.070
about the different forms of activism,

01:16:29.070 --> 01:16:32.480
there's legal activism, around whether

01:16:32.480 --> 01:16:35.920
children can attend schools,
there's labor activism

01:16:35.920 --> 01:16:40.130
that's very direct about how to organize

01:16:40.130 --> 01:16:42.720
unauthorized workers which at the time,

01:16:42.720 --> 01:16:44.950
had been considered to be unorganizable

01:16:44.950 --> 01:16:48.070
and who were considered
to be the strike breakers,

01:16:48.070 --> 01:16:49.730
oh we don't want undocumented migrants

01:16:49.730 --> 01:16:53.760
was the common reasoning
because they will come

01:16:53.760 --> 01:16:55.440
and they will act as strike breakers,

01:16:55.440 --> 01:16:57.890
so it was a first instance when they said

01:16:57.890 --> 01:17:00.760
actually, we can organize
them and we can do so

01:17:00.760 --> 01:17:03.403
by going to Mexico and
organizing them there.

01:17:06.274 --> 01:17:07.743
- [Steve] Any other questions?

01:17:10.584 --> 01:17:11.830
Ana, I wanna give you the last word,

01:17:11.830 --> 01:17:14.150
I wanna first thank you, I mean,

01:17:14.150 --> 01:17:17.440
we need more of the
voices of more historians

01:17:17.440 --> 01:17:19.510
thinking about these things, we're clearly

01:17:19.510 --> 01:17:21.785
in a moment in our collective histories

01:17:21.785 --> 01:17:24.400
in North America in which
so many of the issues

01:17:24.400 --> 01:17:27.140
that you're talking about are certainly

01:17:27.140 --> 01:17:30.460
in the newspaper, they're the subject

01:17:30.460 --> 01:17:32.940
of Human Rights Watch reports,

01:17:32.940 --> 01:17:34.910
and they're very much on our minds

01:17:34.910 --> 01:17:38.130
as we think about the wellbeing of

01:17:38.130 --> 01:17:39.720
our neighbors, our neighborhoods,

01:17:39.720 --> 01:17:42.480
our family members, and as somebody

01:17:42.480 --> 01:17:45.070
as a historian who
thinks about this period

01:17:45.070 --> 01:17:48.170
between 1965 and 1986, and makes

01:17:48.170 --> 01:17:49.780
what I think is a convincing argument

01:17:49.780 --> 01:17:52.857
that so much of what
we're dealing with today,

01:17:52.857 --> 01:17:57.250
in 2018, was framed by the period

01:17:57.250 --> 01:17:59.658
in which, the period that you document

01:17:59.658 --> 01:18:03.760
in this late 20th century moment,

01:18:03.760 --> 01:18:06.180
I want just to ask you to close by saying

01:18:06.180 --> 01:18:09.020
a word if you can about how understanding

01:18:09.020 --> 01:18:13.940
the history from 1965 to 1986 has shaped

01:18:13.940 --> 01:18:17.073
the way in which you
see developments today?

01:18:18.400 --> 01:18:19.363
- Yeah, so.

01:18:22.380 --> 01:18:24.800
In some ways we think about the history

01:18:24.800 --> 01:18:28.410
of Mexican migration as thinking

01:18:28.410 --> 01:18:31.160
oh they've always come, but actually,

01:18:31.160 --> 01:18:34.290
Mexican migration,
unauthorized Mexican migration,

01:18:34.290 --> 01:18:38.060
really starts up and
continues uninterrupted

01:18:38.060 --> 01:18:41.510
until 2008, but it really
starts after the end

01:18:41.510 --> 01:18:43.570
of the Bracero program.

01:18:43.570 --> 01:18:47.620
And the reasons why it starts is because

01:18:47.620 --> 01:18:49.640
people had been given an opportunity

01:18:49.640 --> 01:18:52.380
to come through the Bracero program

01:18:52.380 --> 01:18:54.816
and that was taken away, so part of what

01:18:54.816 --> 01:18:57.380
I think is important to think about

01:18:57.380 --> 01:19:00.870
is how the United States has historically

01:19:00.870 --> 01:19:05.530
recruited people to come,
created a culture of migration

01:19:05.530 --> 01:19:07.530
and then when they no longer want,

01:19:07.530 --> 01:19:10.466
when the economy or the rhetoric

01:19:10.466 --> 01:19:13.830
when they need a scapegoat, they decide

01:19:13.830 --> 01:19:16.742
to close the door and blame Mexicans.

01:19:16.742 --> 01:19:18.450
So that's one of the things

01:19:18.450 --> 01:19:22.060
that I constantly think
about, the other thing

01:19:22.060 --> 01:19:25.250
that I think about is
how the same stereotypes

01:19:25.250 --> 01:19:27.120
are repeated over and over again

01:19:27.120 --> 01:19:29.836
for example, the idea that
migrants abuse welfare.

01:19:29.836 --> 01:19:33.830
It starts in the 1970s
because there's this idea

01:19:33.830 --> 01:19:35.610
that there's a welfare crisis,

01:19:35.610 --> 01:19:37.700
but it continues even up to today,

01:19:37.700 --> 01:19:42.113
so there's just the
recycling of stereotypes.

01:19:44.890 --> 01:19:46.990
And then there is another phenomenon

01:19:46.990 --> 01:19:49.481
that really interests me
which is this idea that

01:19:49.481 --> 01:19:53.280
the way populations look really change.

01:19:53.280 --> 01:19:58.120
So as I said, up until
1986, it was primarily

01:19:58.120 --> 01:19:59.930
men who were coming,
children were not coming,

01:19:59.930 --> 01:20:02.190
women were not coming, that switches

01:20:02.190 --> 01:20:03.940
and now when we think about migration,

01:20:03.940 --> 01:20:05.630
we think about families,
we think about family

01:20:05.630 --> 01:20:07.780
separation in the sense of deportations

01:20:07.780 --> 01:20:10.480
but actually, we're seeing another wave

01:20:10.480 --> 01:20:13.650
which is that since 2008, there's a net

01:20:13.650 --> 01:20:15.520
out migration of Mexicans.

01:20:15.520 --> 01:20:17.640
Mexicans are not coming
to the United States

01:20:17.640 --> 01:20:19.250
when we think about these patterns,

01:20:19.250 --> 01:20:21.470
more Mexicans are leaving than are coming

01:20:21.470 --> 01:20:24.300
and that's extremely
important to understand

01:20:24.300 --> 01:20:27.261
that these waves, that this notion

01:20:27.261 --> 01:20:29.830
that there are waves of people coming

01:20:29.830 --> 01:20:32.170
are just not true,
Mexicans are not coming,

01:20:32.170 --> 01:20:34.860
more Mexicans are leaving and yet

01:20:34.860 --> 01:20:37.620
it is Mexicans, even
though the populations

01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:41.860
targeted are now primarily
Central Americans,

01:20:41.860 --> 01:20:43.330
they are the ones whose bodies

01:20:43.330 --> 01:20:45.950
are being targeted, but rhetorically,

01:20:45.950 --> 01:20:49.420
it's still Mexicans, we still talk about

01:20:50.810 --> 01:20:52.460
President Trump still talks about,

01:20:52.460 --> 01:20:54.713
I wanna say we 'cause I'm
not talking about that,

01:20:55.620 --> 01:20:57.340
how you know Mexicans are rapists

01:20:57.340 --> 01:21:00.622
and criminals, so that continues

01:21:00.622 --> 01:21:03.160
to be part of the rhetoric even though

01:21:03.160 --> 01:21:05.566
that is no longer the reality

01:21:05.566 --> 01:21:07.690
and now in terms of what I think

01:21:07.690 --> 01:21:10.466
is the role of the Mexican government,

01:21:10.466 --> 01:21:13.500
part of what historicizing has made me do

01:21:13.500 --> 01:21:15.940
is consider the role of
the Mexican government

01:21:15.940 --> 01:21:18.470
and I'm also extremely
interested in seeing

01:21:18.470 --> 01:21:21.037
what the Mexican government is doing

01:21:21.037 --> 01:21:22.930
in terms of Central
Americans who are going

01:21:22.930 --> 01:21:25.220
through and that is what I think we need

01:21:25.220 --> 01:21:27.940
to be talking about now, how the position

01:21:27.940 --> 01:21:30.150
of the Mexican government
towards Central Americans

01:21:30.150 --> 01:21:33.610
has changed, so part of what I discovered

01:21:33.610 --> 01:21:37.100
while doing this research
was that the United States

01:21:37.100 --> 01:21:39.230
told Mexican officials, you want Mexicans

01:21:39.230 --> 01:21:41.920
to continue coming to the United States,

01:21:41.920 --> 01:21:44.380
okay, then close the border
to Central Americans,

01:21:44.380 --> 01:21:45.850
don't let Central Americans come,

01:21:45.850 --> 01:21:48.070
'cause we're really scared
of Central Americans,

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:49.761
so if you stop Central
Americans from coming

01:21:49.761 --> 01:21:53.020
we won't have to take such strong actions

01:21:53.020 --> 01:21:55.340
at the border, which will obviously also

01:21:55.340 --> 01:21:59.430
affect Mexicans, right,
so now we can look at

01:21:59.430 --> 01:22:01.936
that history and say why is Mexico still

01:22:01.936 --> 01:22:04.880
criminalizing Central Americans,

01:22:04.880 --> 01:22:07.460
when Mexicans did get criminalized
when that didn't change

01:22:07.460 --> 01:22:09.583
so that's part of what
I'm thinking about now.

01:22:10.688 --> 01:22:12.670
- And it seems to me also, I mean

01:22:12.670 --> 01:22:15.050
one of your major arguments, I guess

01:22:15.050 --> 01:22:18.530
is that the Mexican
government had historically

01:22:18.530 --> 01:22:20.935
sacrificed its own citizenry in a kind

01:22:20.935 --> 01:22:25.150
of modernization project
tired to the Green Revolution

01:22:25.150 --> 01:22:27.023
in which it sacrificed the ideals of

01:22:27.023 --> 01:22:29.691
the Mexican revolution
to which it professed

01:22:29.691 --> 01:22:34.240
repeatedly in the late 20th Century,

01:22:34.240 --> 01:22:37.010
so perhaps, what you may be saying is

01:22:37.010 --> 01:22:38.700
that it may be no surprise given

01:22:38.700 --> 01:22:42.480
the Mexican government's failure

01:22:42.480 --> 01:22:44.930
to live up to the terms
of the revolutionary

01:22:44.930 --> 01:22:46.850
constitution in the late 20th century

01:22:46.850 --> 01:22:48.500
that today, the Mexican government

01:22:48.500 --> 01:22:51.730
may be also engaging
in a classist politics

01:22:51.730 --> 01:22:52.940
vis a vis Central Americans,

01:22:52.940 --> 01:22:56.380
a racist politics vis a
vis Central Americans,

01:22:56.380 --> 01:22:58.200
a king of modernization project

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:00.990
tied to the United States
and a migration project

01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:03.922
tied to the migration of the United States

01:23:03.922 --> 01:23:07.300
that, in a sense, does gesture back

01:23:07.300 --> 01:23:08.910
to the very period in
which you're discussing.

01:23:08.910 --> 01:23:11.060
- That's why he was my
advisor, absolutely.

01:23:12.020 --> 01:23:13.440
- I did not wanna give
myself the last word,

01:23:13.440 --> 01:23:15.840
join me, though, in
thanking and applauding

01:23:15.840 --> 01:23:19.050
Ana Minian.
(audience applauds)

01:23:19.050 --> 01:23:21.343
Thank you, Ana.
- Thank you, everyone.

01:23:23.721 --> 01:23:26.138
(closing music)

